{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1711,1935p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":" :where [?d :yin.ledger/op :derive] [?d :yin.ledger/output ?seg] [?d :yin.ledger/input ?tree]\n        [?x :yin.ledger/op :expand] [?x :yin.ledger/output ?tree] [?x :yin.ledger/input ?src]]\n```\n\n---\n\n## §9 Migration surface\n\n### 9.1 The boundary, not a sweep\n\nThe walker stays on map ASTs exactly as built, retaining named variable evaluation. What changes is the\nboundary around them — the projection in, the loader and the readers of\nrows behind it. The semantics do not change; the boundary does.\n\n+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Site                                             | Change                                                                                                              |\n+==================================================+=====================================================================================================================+\n| walker, `ast_walker.cljc`                        | Retains named variable evaluation (`:name`). `:lambda` arm binds by name. Does not use De Bruijn numbering. Adds `:stream/close` arm (§3.2).                                                                                                      |\n+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| loader, new                                      | validate rows (§7.4), reconstruct the map AST: `rows → map` (§6.1), the successor of `datoms->ast`                  |\n|                                                  | (`v2.cljc:494-559`) with content addresses in place of allocated ids                                                |\n+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| linearizer,                                      | reads the row relation through the same `get-attr`-over-an-index shape it has today (`lower-node`'s `(get-attr e    |\n| `linearize.cljc:87-148`,                         | :yin/type)` becomes a lookup by row id); `ast-children` becomes a table lookup of `node`/`nodes` slot positions;    |\n| `:230-242`                                       | `lower` takes a row set and returns a vector plus the §5.3 provenance table; emits `:var name`, `:global name`, and `:closure params body`; `lower-ast` (`:245-252`) goes away     |\n+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| codec, `v2.cljc:372-559`                         | becomes `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`, the projection pair of §6.5 mapping Universal AST to Canonical Rows/Datoms.            |\n|                                                  | `:yin/address` added; `:global` node added; retains `:yin/params` on lambda and `:yin/name` on variable |\n+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `code/mnemonics`, `code/well-formed?`,           | `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`) adds `:global`. `well-formed?` keeps judging datom batches on the projection path before projection; the shared vector validator of §7.5 runs on   |\n| `code.cljc:12-180`                               | both paths after it                                                                                                 |\n+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `semantic/load-image`,                           | decodes from the vector directly on the primary path; positional frame binding for `:closure`; `:global` opcode resolves store → primitives → modules; decodes the new operands (`:var name`, `:global name`, `:closure params body`); both paths call the §7.5 validator   |\n| `semantic.cljc:524-596`                          |                                                                                                                     |\n+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| frontends                                        | keep emitting map ASTs to the stream (as named universal ASTs). They do not perform the tuple projection. |\n| `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`,                    | All projection to Semantic Tuples and side-tables is deferred to the Encoder Observer (`yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`).        |\n| `python.cljc`, `php.cljc`                        |                                                                                                                     |\n|                                                  | never survive the boundary. The r7 adapter's ordering rules become the standing contract: first reject any `:macro? |\n|                                                  | true` lambda that is not the value operand of a `yin/def` as `:stray-macro-lambda`, then turn each admitted one into|\n|                                                  | a declaration at its tree index and path, then strip the flag                                                       |\n+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| datom-batch adapter,                             | for media that carry datoms: derives `:yin/harvest` from entity order and entity identity while inlining, recording |\n| standing                                         | every `[j path]` at which each definition entity is emitted (the `datoms->ast` recursion, `v2.cljc:494-559`,        |\n|                                                  | extended to carry the path and the entity id, since today it tracks neither); one entity with several paths becomes |\n|                                                  | one occurrence group; the adapter assigns no `:decl` values (§8.5)                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nThe v1 lineage (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/ast_walker_test.cljc`,\n`test/yin/vm/ast_conversion_test.cljc`, `runtime_regression_test.cljc`) is\noutside this design; it stays on its own map path until it is retired.\n\n### 9.2 The observer lane: an integration item to scope, not a solved detail\n\nProgram input reaches an evaluator through\n`dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:217-247`),\nwhich is shape-agnostic: it hands each observed batch to a\ncomposition-supplied `load`. The per-evaluator loaders are chosen in\n`yin.repl.core/program-loaders` (`repl/v2/core.cljc:63-68`) and today\naccept datom batches; the REPL's own eval path still converts a\nsemantic-VM AST through `ast->datoms` before it travels\n(`repl/v2/core.cljc:444-449`). Two things about the row lane are\n**unverified**:\n\n- **The batch shape.** Whether one batch is one tree's row set, one\n  `{:yin/code :yin/declarations}` value (§8.5), one vector, or a\n  collection of them is\n  a medium contract this design has not exercised end to end; §7.1 assumes\n  one canonical value per batch. The shape must be fixed with the REPL\n  shell and the `yin.vm.stream-observer` tests before the loaders are\n  switched.\n- **The program-input predicate.** `engine/executable-program-datom?`\n  (`engine.cljc:511-513`) decides what counts as program input by testing\n  the attribute namespace of an `[e a v t m]` row, and\n  `append-program-datoms` (`engine.cljc:590-612`) builds on it. A row\n  batch has no attribute slot. A repository search on 2026-09-14 found no\n  caller of the v2 `append-program-datoms` beyond its own overload, so a\n  row counterpart is not presumed necessary; whether any live\n  composition reaches the predicate is what the implementation phase\n  establishes before deciding.\n\nBoth are integration items to scope in the implementation phase.\n\n### 9.3 What stays on datoms, and why\n\nThis is §1's datom-layer jobs\nseen from the migration side:\n\n- **The ledger, every ref, and every ledger event** (§8): mutability,\n  history, provenance, and custody are datom properties, and the\n  transactor is what possesses them.\n- **`dao.space.index` and published covered indexes**: they index datoms,\n  and the scale path of §6.5 goes through them.\n- **The macro expander's event log** (§8.4): events are entities with\n  attributes, one per attempt; only their values changed kind.\n- **The datom projection of code** (§6.5): kept for the three query classes\n  it alone serves, regenerable from the content at any time.\n- **Execution traces** (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.6): state over time is a\n  datom stream by design and is untouched.\n\n---\n\n## §10 Acceptance blockers and open items\n\n1. **Type-preserving canonical encoding in `dao.jing`** — declared,\n   inherited, and **blocking every identity and deduplication use**\n   (§4.2). `order-normalize` (`jing.cljc:45-64`) coerces every sequential\n   value to a vector and `pr-str` drops metadata, so lists, seqs, and\n   metadata-bearing vectors collide with plain vectors. No restricted\n   value domain is claimed; the round-2 list-rejection rule is withdrawn\n   as unsound. Rows, vectors, ledger-record maps, row\n   deduplication, DAG sharing, process-local caches, content resolution by\n   address, and ledger rows are all blocked alike until `dao.jing`'s\n   encoding passes the conformance pairs of §4.2. This design does not\n   close the item.\n2. **The store-key domain is the `data` domain** (§2.2): every validator,\n   extraction query, and store-slice encoding must use that one\n   definition. This document now states it uniformly; the implementation\n   phase must not reintroduce a narrower one, and the corpus must contain\n   a numeric key beside the keyword one.\n3. **Complete occurrence identity** (§2.5, §5.3, §8.4) — design work\n   with stated mechanisms, not yet implementable. Source origins\n   `[:source medium batch j]` must be supplied to the lowering and\n   recorded in instruction provenance and initial expansion events;\n   nested expansion events must carry both the declared-ref parent link\n   and the parent record address, and the record must be minted after\n   the parent's. The medium identity coordinate is whatever the\n   composition names its program medium by; this document does not fix\n   its shape, and that shape must be fixed before an origin can be\n   written. The expander incarnation (§8.4.1) is a composition-minted\n   random token, never a log-allocated id or a stream position; its\n   uniqueness is the composition's warranty, discharged by the token's\n   randomness, and no transactor is claimed to provide it. The\n   `:incarnation` field of `ctx` and the token-minting step at\n   construction are additions to `yin.vm.macro.md`'s `ctx` and expander\n   construction that this document cannot make there.\n4. **Macro batch and admission preservation** (§8.5) — design work with\n   stated mechanisms. The ordered batch, the admission-order harvest\n   catalogue of occurrence groups, the derived macro-only declaration\n   catalogue, its admission validation, the validate-before-strip rule,\n   and the adapter's path-and-entity tracking during inlining must exist\n   before any tuple program containing a macro definition can be\n   admitted. The legacy datom-batch contract is\n   preserved only through the adapter-derived catalogue; tuple-native\n   frontends operate\n   under the separate declaration-order contract stated there. The v2\n   expander itself does not exist yet (`src/cljc/yin/vm/` has no\n   `macro.cljc`) and is not a blocker for tuple evaluation without\n   macros; §8.4's event shape is specified against `yin.vm.macro.md`, not\n   against code.\n5. **Conservative dependency completion** (§7.7.2, §7.7.3) — design\n   work with stated mechanisms. Global name discharge requires searching the\n   store slice by key or resolving via primitive/module profiles (§7.7.2);\n   convergence is over work items and all dependency facts, with the\n   positional-environment context abstraction for values. Neither analysis exists in code;\n   until they do, an emitter may only report `:incomplete`.\n6. **Effect normalization** (§7.7.1): the footprint table must be\n   published with the execution-contract stamp, and the tree/segment\n   requirement-set equality must be tested over the corpus. FFI ops are a\n   receiver capability requirement, never a store-slice requirement.\n7. **The lowering profile** (§5.2.1): `\"ast-v1\"` must be published as a\n   pinned document before any derivation record is written; until then\n   no derivation can be verified, only content integrity (§5.2.2).\n8. **UCF §7.6.1 needs two amendments** (§7.7.2, §7.7.3): its\n   any-carried-environment satisfaction sentence is unsound and must be\n   replaced by the store-slice-by-key / profile rule of §7.7.2 (an environment never\n   discharges a name), and its address-per-segment fixed\n   point must become the work-item fixed point. This document cannot edit\n   UCF.\n9. **The observer-lane batch shape and the program-input predicate**\n   (§9.2) — integration items with no design answer in this document. The\n   §8.5 ordered batch is a candidate shape for the AST medium, not a\n   verified one.\n10. **Ledger event representation on the transactor** (§8.2): the\n    event-entity form is legal under `local-datom?` as written, but the\n    map-`m` provenance path (`transact.cljc:15-29`) and the metadata-op\n    interpreter `dao.space.schema.datomic.md` §4 names are not exercised by\n    this design's tests yet. The naming rows need neither; the provenance\n    link from a naming fact to its event does.\n11. **The primitive-profile registry** — inherited from UCF §7.11. Every\n    retained name obligation is checked by profile under UCF §7.5.2, and\n    §7.7.2 reads callable effects from profiles; until\n    `yin.vm/primitives` (`v2.cljc:100-132`) is published with profiles,\n    dependency closure reports names and `:incomplete`, not satisfiable\n    bindings.\n12. **Per-lambda subterm addressing** — dissolved by the flat-row form\n    (§4.3): every node is addressed by construction, and the remaining\n    one-segment-per-lambda lowering question is a §5.2.1 profile concern.\n13. **The round-trip law** (§7.2, part 1) — a first-class conformance\n    obligation, not a blocker: for every corpus program, `map → rows →\n    map` is the identity on the canonical map AST and `rows → map →\n    rows` is the identity on the rows, and both directions must be\n    tested over the corpus before any row set is treated as canonical.\n    The corpus already lives as map ASTs in the test tree, so the test\n    is writable now.\n14. **`yin.vm.semantic.md` Instruction Grammar Amendment** (§5.1) — the `ast-v1` lowering\n    profile (§5.2.1) pins the §5.3 table of `yin.vm.semantic.md`, which is currently\n    the name-based one. That document's §2.4 and §5.3 must be amended to reflect the\n    Named variable instruction grammar\n    and the positional binding rule of §7.7.2 (under-arity call → missing slots `nil`; extra arguments beyond `arity` are dropped)\n    before any derivation record is written.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"The idempotency claim also needs narrowing. The index design makes allocation deterministic for re-executing the same pure fold from the same pre-state, but resolution facts do not deduplicate a batch that is appended twice; a second append has a new observer ordinal and correctly creates new durable entities. I’m checking the plan’s exact wording and acceptance gates now, including cross-topic atomicity and the canonical-hash blocker."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/d463453f-497a-4a75-a0bd-20cd63ac6838/vm_semantic_bytecode_migration_plan.md && nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '140,180p;250,315p;350,430p;540,590p;810,845p;980,1035p;1718,1765p;1780,1920p' && nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md | sed -n '150,260p;285,410p;700,790p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/d463453f-497a-4a75-a0bd-20cd63ac6838/vm_semantic_bytecode_migration_plan.md && nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '140,180p;250,315p;350,430p;540,590p;810,845p;980,1035p;1718,1765p;1780,1920p' && nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md | sed -n '150,260p;285,410p;700,790p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t## Goal Description\n     2\tThe `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` architecture document establishes that our LISP ASTs must be lowered into flat, content-addressed tuple rows (`[id tag & slots]`) which act as semantic bytecode. \n     3\t\n     4\tThe goal of this plan is to implement the execution layer migration, establishing a fully reactive compilation and execution pipeline over `dao.stream`.\n     5\t\n     6\t**Architectural Clarification:** De Bruijn indexing is entirely deferred from the Semantic layer. The Universal Map AST and Semantic Bytecode Tuples strictly retain their named variables to perfectly preserve original programming semantics. De Bruijn indexing will be introduced strictly in the later **Register VM** compilation phase for execution optimization.\n     7\t\n     8\t### The Reactive Compilation Pipeline\n     9\tEmbracing the \"everything is a stream\" architecture, the pipeline is composed of purely stateless stream transformers. \n    10\t\n    11\t```mermaid\n    12\tflowchart TD\n    13\t    A[Source Text Topic] -->|Observe| Y[Yang Compiler]\n    14\t    Y -->|Emit Named Map AST| M(dao.stream: Map AST Topic)\n    15\t    \n    16\t    M -->|Observe| AW[AST Walker]\n    17\t    AW -->|Execute Directly| Exec2(State/IO)\n    18\t    \n    19\t    \n    20\t    M -->|Observe| ENC[Encoder Observer]\n    21\t    ENC -->|yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode| ENC\n    22\t    \n    23\t    ENC -->|Emit Batch Envelope & Rows| S1(dao.stream: AST Medium)\n    24\t    ENC -->|Emit side-table datoms with negative tempids| S2(dao.stream: Datoms Topic)\n    25\t    \n    26\t    S2 -->|Observe Datoms| IDX[dao.space.index in :unresolved mode]\n    27\t    IDX -->|Materialize| Rel2[(dao.space) Datom Ledger]\n    28\t    \n    29\t    S1 -->|Observe Rows| AST[AST Indexer]\n    30\t    AST -->|Materialize| Rel1[($ast) Row Relation]\n    31\t    \n    32\t    S1 -->|Observe Rows| VM[Semantic VM]\n    33\t    VM -->|Internal: lower under profile| IV(Named Instruction Vector)\n    34\t    IV -->|Execute| Exec1(State/IO)\n    35\t    \n    36\t    S1 -->|Observe Rows| RVM[Register VM Compiler]\n    37\t    RVM -->|Compute De Bruijn Indices| S3(dao.stream: Register VM Bytecode)\n    38\t    S3 -->|Execute| Exec3(Register VM)\n    39\t```\n    40\t\n    41\t## Proposed Changes\n    42\t\n    43\t### 1. Yang Compiler (Frontend)\n    44\t- The frontends remain purely focused on language semantics. They observe source text from `dao.stream` and emit a parsed Map AST (with named variables) back to `dao.stream`. They have zero knowledge of tuples, datoms, or tempids.\n    45\t\n    46\t### 2. AST Walker (Direct Evaluator)\n    47\t- The `ast_walker` directly observes the Map AST topic and evaluates the code immediately. It continues to bind and evaluate variables by name.\n    48\t\n    49\t### 3. The Encoder Observer & `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`\n    50\t- Implement a staged-output **Encoder Observer** that listens to the Map AST stream.\n    51\t- The observer uses a new `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` function to translate the Map AST into the Semantic Tuple representation, strictly preserving the named variables.\n    52\t- **Output 1 - AST Medium:** The encoder emits an ordered **Batch Envelope** (`{:yin/batch :yin/root :yin/declarations :yin/harvest}`) containing the Semantic Bytecode tuples. Because this bytecode is stored as flat, relational tuples, this is the exact layer where `dao.space.query/q` executes Datalog queries over the codebase.\n    53\t- **Output 2 - Side Tables (Tempids):** The encoder emits its side-table metadata directly to the Datoms Topic using batch-local negative tempids. The Transactor is intentionally bypassed because it does not support tempids or identity resolution.\n    54\t\n    55\t### 4. Semantic VM (Lowering & Execution)\n    56\t- **Direct AST Medium Observation:** The Semantic VM observes the AST Medium directly.\n    57\t- **Local Lowering:** The loader uses function composition to run the `lower` step on the row set, producing the Instruction Vector. \n    58\t- **Execution:** The VM execution loop runs the lowered Instruction Vector. It binds and evaluates variables by name, perfectly reflecting the Map AST semantics.\n    59\t\n    60\t### 5. Register VM (Future Optimization)\n    61\t- A downstream **Register VM Compiler** will observe the AST Medium. \n    62\t- This compiler's exclusive responsibility is converting named variables into highly-optimized **De Bruijn indices** and outputting Register VM Bytecode.\n    63\t\n    64\t### 6. Dedicated AST Indexer & Space Index Session\n    65\t- **AST Indexer:** A `dao.stream` observer that consumes the Batch Envelopes and materializes the `$ast` row relation without collapsing rows on ID equality (pending the `dao.jing` fix).\n    66\t- **Space Index:** A `dao.space.index/session` observes the Datoms Topic natively in **`:unresolved` mode**. It acts as the ID allocator, dynamically resolving the batch-local negative tempids into durable IDs and asserting `[:dao.space.index/batch n]` resolution facts to guarantee idempotency on retries.\n    67\t\n    68\t## Verification Plan\n    69\t\n    70\t### Automated Tests (clj, cljs, cljd)\n    71\tAll tests must pass across all three hosts (`bb test:clj`, `bb test:cljs`, `bb test:cljd`).\n    72\t1. **Round-Trip Law Test:** Verify that `map → rows → map` is identical on the canonical map AST.\n    73\t2. **Malformed Input Refusals:** Ensure both row and vector validators correctly reject a predefined set of malformed structures (e.g., bounds violations, shape violations).\n    74\t3. **AST Querying Test:** Ensure `dao.space.query/q` correctly queries the `$ast` relation joined with the `dao.space` ledger. Ensure test suites explicitly block until both the AST Indexer and Space Index sessions have caught up before querying.\n   140\t## §2 The AST tuple grammar\n   141\t\n   142\t### 2.1 Form\n   143\t\n   144\tThe map AST is the code's own representation — the **semantic layer** (the Universal AST). A\n   145\tnode is a map of its tag and its named fields:\n   146\t\n   147\t```clojure\n   148\t{:type :lambda,\n   149\t :arity 1,\n   150\t :body {:type :application,\n   151\t        :operator {:type :global, :name +},\n   152\t        :operands [{:type :variable, :index 0} {:type :literal, :value 1}],\n   153\t        :tail? true}}\n   154\t```\n   155\t\n   156\tThe fields are what the walker dispatches on today (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:333-470`), though the walker's `:variable` and `:lambda` arms must be adapted to resolve indices against a positional environment and bind `arity`, while `:global` resolves through the store/primitives/modules (`engine.cljc:46-58`).\n   157\t\n   158\tThe map AST is never hashed directly, never stored, never\n   159\tshipped. It exists on both sides of storage — frontend-side, where\n   160\tfrontends emit it, and machine-side, where the loader reconstructs it\n   161\t(§7.1) — and it is ephemeral and per-consumer, like an image: each\n   162\tconsumer's map is its own value, though conceptually they all represent the canonical Universal AST (the semantic representation).\n   163\t\n   164\tThe **canonical stored artifact** is the **flat per-node row projection** of the map\n   165\tAST, `project : map-ast → rows`, one row per node (the bytecode linearization):\n   166\t\n   167\t```clojure\n   168\t[A :lambda 1 B]\n   169\t[B :application C [D E] true]\n   170\t[C :global +]\n   171\t[D :variable 0]\n   172\t[E :literal 1]          ; A..E are :segment/sha256-… row ids\n   173\t```\n   174\t\n   175\tA row's **body** is `[tag & slots]`: the slots are the node's field\n   176\tvalues in the §2.3 order — a child slot holds the child row's id, a\n   177\t`nodes` slot holds the ordered vector of the children's ids, and every\n   178\tother slot holds its data unchanged. The row's id is\n   179\t`(dao.jing/segment-key body)` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:217-225`). The stored payload in `dao.jing` is exactly the flat body `[tag & slots]`. When queried or streamed, the system reconstructs the full logical row by prepending the returned address, yielding `[id tag & slots]`. The id is an address envelope outside the hashed payload. The\n   180\tid is therefore merkle: a parent's id commits to its children's ids\n   250\t\n   251\t### 2.3 The table\n   252\t\n   253\tThe tag set is the walker's dispatch set reconciled with the codec's\n   254\tinventory (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:413-478`) under the two boundary calls\n   255\tof §3. The `Body arity` column counts the body items (tag plus slots). A full row's count is its body arity plus one (for the ID). This table is the **projection and reconstruction\n   256\tdictionary**: the slot list of a tag, in order, is exactly the map's key\n   257\tset beyond `:type` — the arity ↔ key-set correspondence — so the one\n   258\ttable fixes the row's positions and the map's fields together. Position\n   259\t`i` of a row body holds the value of the field the *Slots* column names\n   260\tat `i`; projection reads the positions, reconstruction reads the names,\n   261\tand neither side may disagree with the other.\n   262\t\n   263\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   264\t| Tag                     | Body arity | Row body                             | Slots                    | Slot kinds       | Walker arm         | Codec arm       |\n   265\t+=========================+============+======================================+==========================+==================+====================+=================+\n   266\t| :literal                | 2          | [:literal value]                     | value                    | data             | ast_walker.cljc:360| v2.cljc:414-415 |\n   267\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   268\t| :variable               | 2          | [:variable name]                     | name                     | sym              | changes: index -> name   | changes: `:yin/index` -> `:yin/name` |\n   269\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   270\t| :global                 | 2          | [:global name]                       | name                     | sym              | added by this design | added by this design |\n   271\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   272\t| :lambda                 | 3          | [:lambda params body]                | params body              | data, node       | changes: arity -> params | changes: arity -> params |\n   273\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   274\t| :application            | 4          | [:application operator operands      | operator operands tail?  | node, nodes, bool| :371-376           | :424-429        |\n   275\t|                         |            | tail?]                               |                          |                  |                    |                 |\n   276\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   277\t| :if                     | 4          | [:if test consequent alternate]      | test consequent alternate| node, node, node | :377-381           | :435-441        |\n   278\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   279\t| :dao.stream.apply/call  | 3          | [:dao.stream.apply/call op operands] | op operands              | kw, nodes        | :382-394           | :430-434        |\n   280\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   281\t| :vm/gensym              | 2          | [:vm/gensym prefix]                  | prefix                   | str              | :395-400           | :443-444        |\n   282\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   283\t| :vm/store-get           | 2          | [:vm/store-get key]                  | key                      | key              | :401               | :445-446        |\n   284\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   285\t| :vm/store-put           | 3          | [:vm/store-put key val]              | key val                  | key, data        | :402-409           | :447-449        |\n   286\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   287\t| :vm/current-continuation| 1          | [:vm/current-continuation]           | —                        | —                | :422-427           | :474-475        |\n   288\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   289\t| :vm/park                | 1          | [:vm/park]                           | —                        | —                | :428-430           | :469            |\n   290\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   291\t| :vm/resume              | 3          | [:vm/resume parked-id val]           | parked-id val            | kw, node         | :431-438           | :470-473        |\n   292\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   293\t| :stream/make            | 2          | [:stream/make buffer]                | buffer                   | int              | :439-447           | :451-453        |\n   294\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   295\t| :stream/put             | 3          | [:stream/put target val]             | target val               | node, node       | :448-454           | :454-458        |\n   296\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   297\t| :stream/cursor          | 2          | [:stream/cursor source]              | source                   | node             | :455-461           | :459-461        |\n   298\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   299\t| :stream/next            | 2          | [:stream/next source]                | source                   | node             | :462-468           | :462-464        |\n   300\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   301\t| :stream/close           | 2          | [:stream/close source]               | source                   | node             | added by §3.2      | :465-467        |\n   302\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   303\t\n   304\t**Named Variables:** A `:variable` row's `name` retains the original string/symbol from the AST. (De Bruijn computation is deferred to the Register VM phase).\n   305\t\n   306\t`:vm/store-update` is not a tag (§3.1). `:yin/macro-expand` is not a tag:\n   307\tthe walker has no arm for it and the linearizer rejects it\n   308\t(`linearize.cljc:19-22`); expansion happens before code reaches this\n   309\tgrammar (`yin.vm.macro.md`, decision 1).\n   310\t\n   311\tThe `:vm/store-put` value slot is data, not a node: the walker stores\n   312\t`(:val node)` without evaluating it (`ast_walker.cljc:402-404`) and the\n   313\tcodec emits it as `:yin/value` (`v2.cljc:449`). The `:vm/resume` value slot\n   314\tis a node: the walker evaluates it (`ast_walker.cljc:431-438`).\n   315\t\n   350\t| `:application` / `:dao.stream.apply/call` operands | `[]`                                | walker `ast_walker.cljc:383`                                                |\n   351\t+----------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   352\t\n   353\t### 2.5 Exclusions, occurrences, and side tables\n   354\t\n   355\tExclusion is a rule of the projection boundary, the same rule §2.4 states\n   356\tfor saturation: these facts are stripped map→rows and never re-derived\n   357\trows→map. The map AST a frontend emits carries them; no row does:\n   358\t\n   359\t- **Source positions**, **parameter/variable names**, and any frontend metadata (`:yang/*` keys, Clojure\n   360\t  reader metadata on symbols). The boundary projection retains named variables in the canonical tuples. Secondary side tables are still emitted for source position tracking, but the bytecode itself preserves semantics.\n   361\t- **`:macro?`** and `:phase-policy`. `yang.clojure` sets them on defmacro\n   362\t  lambdas (`clojure.cljc:436-438`) and the codec persists `:yin/macro?`\n   363\t  (`v2.cljc:419-420`, schema `v2.cljc:312`). No evaluator reads them\n   364\t  (`ast_walker.cljc:20-23`). The expander needs the fact that a particular\n   365\t  definition is a macro; that fact travels beside the tree as an\n   366\t  occurrence-bound declaration (§8.5), not as a slot.\n   367\t- **`:eid`** (§4.4).\n   368\t- **`:yin/root`.** A tree is its own root; the batch or the ref that names\n   369\t  the tree names the root. No root fact exists in a row — the root row's\n   370\t  id is the root fact (§4.1).\n   371\t- **`t`, `m`, provenance.** Every provenance fact the codec or linearizer\n   372\t  writes today (`:yin.code/source`, `:yin.code/derived-from`) becomes a\n   373\t  ledger record (§8) or a side-table entry.\n   374\t\n   375\t**Occurrences.** Content identity names *what*; three further things\n   376\tname *where*, and each is a distinct coordinate:\n   377\t\n   378\t- A **structural path** names a place inside one tree: a vector of slot\n   379\t  indices from the root, with an index into a `nodes` slot written as a\n   380\t  pair `[slot i]`. `[]` is the root; `[2 [1 0]]` is the first operand of\n   381\t  the root's second slot. Slot indices are the row's positions, which the\n   382\t  §2.3 dictionary fixes against the map's key order, so a path reads\n   383\t  identically on both sides of the round trip. A path is a pure function\n   384\t  of the tree, stable across media and hosts. `[root-address path]`\n   385\t  separates two identical subtrees at two places in one tree, and nothing\n   386\t  more.\n   387\t- A **source occurrence** names one admission of one tree: `[medium batch\n   388\t  j]`, the program medium's identity, the batch coordinate on it (the\n   389\t  `:yin/source-batch` of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1), and the **member index**\n   390\t  `j` of the tree within the batch (§8.5's ordered batch carries several\n   391\t  trees). Two identical trees parsed from two files, admitted in two\n   392\t  batches, or sitting at two indices of one batch have one root address\n   393\t  and two source occurrences. A root address alone never identifies a\n   394\t  source, and neither does a batch without its member index.\n   395\t- A **producing event** names one generated tree: the `:expand` event\n   396\t  (§8.4) whose output it is. Two expansion attempts with identical output\n   397\t  have one output address and two producing events. An event has two\n   398\t  identities, used in two places: a **log-local entity id**, which only a\n   399\t  declared reference attribute on the same log may name, and the\n   400\t  **address of its expansion record** (§8.2), which is portable.\n   401\t\n   402\tAn **occurrence key** is therefore `[origin root-address path]`, where\n   403\t`origin` is `[:source medium batch j]` for admitted syntax and\n   404\t`[:expansion record-address]` for generated syntax. Every side table and\n   405\tevery provenance link that must distinguish places, sources, or attempts\n   406\tis keyed by an occurrence key, never by an address alone, and the member\n   407\tindex travels in the origin uniformly: declarations, source positions,\n   408\tinstruction provenance, and initial expansion events all carry the same\n   409\t`[:source medium batch j]`. Joining a position table to a batch table on\n   410\troot address is not a substitute: it yields every batch-position\n   411\tcombination, which is exactly the ambiguity the origin coordinate\n   412\tremoves. A bare log-local event number never appears inside an occurrence\n   413\tkey, because a key travels and the number does not (§8.4).\n   414\t\n   415\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   416\t| Side table             | Row                                                                                                                    | Keeper               |\n   417\t+========================+========================================================================================================================+======================+\n   418\t| source positions       | `[[:source medium batch j] root-address path file line col]`                                                           | the frontend that    |\n   419\t|                        |                                                                                                                        | parsed the file      |\n   420\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   421\t| frontend metadata      | `[origin root-address path key value]`                                                                                 | the frontend         |\n   422\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   423\t| variable names         | `[origin root-address path i name]`: the `i`-th parameter of the `:lambda` at `path` was written `name`; a `:variable` row's display name is the join through its binding lambda's path and index | the frontend         |\n   424\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   425\t| instruction provenance | `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]`                                                                        | the lowering (§5.3)  |\n   426\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   427\t| macro declarations     | `[[:source medium batch j] path]`: the definition at `path` of the batch's `j`-th tree is a macro definition; inside   | the frontend,        |\n   428\t|                        | the batch envelope this is written `[j path :yin.macro/definition]` (§8.5)                                             | consumed by the      |\n   429\t|                        |                                                                                                                        | expander             |\n   430\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   540\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   541\t| module manifests pinning AST content          | a manifest names the tree addresses it exports; equal content is equal address across hosts                            |\n   542\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   543\t| lowering as an address-to-address function    | `lower : tree → instruction vector`, and each application of it is one derivation record (§5.2)                        |\n   544\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   545\t\n   546\tThe address is deterministic and operand-order-sensitive: `(add 1 2)` and\n   547\t`(add 2 1)` address differently, because `order-normalize` sorts maps and\n   548\tsets but leaves vectors in place (`jing.cljc:45-64`). Literal maps and sets\n   549\tinside a `data` slot hash order-insensitively, which is correct: they are\n   550\tvalues.\n   551\t\n   552\t### 4.2 The transitional encoder is inherited, and every identity use is blocked on it\n   553\t\n   554\t`segment-key` hashes an order-normalized `pr-str` (`jing.cljc:67-71`,\n   555\t`:203-214`). `order-normalize` coerces **every** sequential value to a\n   556\tvector (`jing.cljc:63`), and `pr-str` prints no metadata. The encoder is\n   557\ttherefore not injective over the `data` domain, and the collisions are not\n   558\tconfined to one collection type:\n   559\t\n   560\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   561\t| Two different values                                   | One address today                  | Why it matters                                                           |\n   562\t+========================================================+====================================+==========================================================================+\n   563\t| `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (1 2)]`              | lists become vectors               | `conj` appends to one and prepends to the other                          |\n   564\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   565\t| `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (seq [1 2])]`        | a seq is sequential but not a list | the same, and a list reject-rule does not see it                         |\n   566\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   567\t| `[:literal ^{:a 1} [1 2]]` and `[:literal ^{:a 2} [1   | metadata is dropped by `pr-str`    | `data` admits plain metadata (`linearize.cljc:58`) and programs may read |\n   568\t| 2]]`                                                   |                                    | it                                                                       |\n   569\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   570\t\n   571\tThis is `dao.jing.md`'s first open item (*Canonical encoding*), inherited\n   572\tunchanged, and it is an acceptance blocker of this design (§10.1).\n   573\t\n   574\t**Every identity and deduplication use is blocked until the encoding is\n   575\tfixed.** No restricted value domain is claimed, and none is admitted: a\n   576\treject-list over collection types does not make address-based identity\n   577\tsafe, because the admitted domain is open (`plain-data?` admits every\n   578\t`coll?`, `linearize.cljc:56`) and the encoder's losses are structural. The\n   579\tblock applies uniformly to every addressed value and every use of an\n   580\taddress as identity: `data` slots of trees, `:const` and `:store-put`\n   581\toperands of vectors, ledger-record maps (§8.2), flat-projection row\n   582\tcollapse (§6.1), DAG sharing (§4.4), process-local address-keyed caches,\n   583\t`dao.jing` reads that resolve an address to a literal value, and every\n   584\tledger row that names an address. Process lifetime is irrelevant. Until\n   585\tthe block lifts, addresses may be *computed* and *carried* for the\n   586\tconformance work of §7.2, but nothing may treat two equal addresses as\n   587\tone value.\n   588\t\n   589\tThe three rows above are part of the **encoder conformance obligation**\n   590\tthat lifts the block: the pinned encoding must address each pair\n   810\t`[segment-address pc tag & ops]`, which adds one to every tag's arity\n   811\tuniformly and keeps per-tag arity fixed. Bare-pc rows from different\n   812\tsegments are never unioned into one relation.\n   813\t\n   814\t### 6.3 `q` over both, and the mechanics it rests on\n   815\t\n   816\t`q` accepts these relations through `query/relation`\n   817\t(`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:153-158`), whose contents are an arbitrary\n   818\tmixed-dimensional tuple collection. A pattern clause is exact-arity\n   819\tpositional unification: `eval-pattern-clause` (`query.cljc:894-907`) parses\n   820\tthe pattern (`query.cljc:419-436`), and on the general path unifies every\n   821\trow through `unify-slots` (`query.cljc:799-809`), which first requires\n   822\t`tuple-shape-matches?` (`query.cljc:439-441`) and then unifies slot by\n   823\tslot. A final `& ?tail` binds the remaining slots as a vector and `& _`\n   824\tignores them (`query.cljc:431-436`, `:807-808`). The 3-slot EAV fast path\n   825\t(`query.cljc:899`) engages only when the source carries a fact index, which\n   826\t`fact-relation` (`query.cljc:172-177`) and the datom views supply; the code\n   827\trelations never do, so every code pattern takes the general path by\n   828\tconstruction.\n   829\t\n   830\tVerified shapes, each of which is a rule a query author may rely on:\n   831\t\n   832\t- **Per-tag selection.** `[?id :variable ?index]` matches only arity-3 rows.\n   833\t- **Joins across arities.** `[?site :application ?op _ _] [?op :global\n   834\t  ?f]` joins an arity-5 row to an arity-3 row on the address.\n   835\t- **Operand membership** goes through a predicate: `[(member? ?operands\n   836\t  ?x)]` with `member?` supplied under `:fns`; predicate arguments must\n   837\t  already be bound.\n   838\t- **Ledger as-of** (§8.6) and **ref-to-code joins** (§6.4) run in one\n   839\t  query, the ref pattern on the fast path and the code pattern on the\n   840\t  general path.\n   841\t\n   842\tTwo gotchas are rules:\n   843\t\n   844\t- `match` (`query.cljc:520-528`) is constant-or-wildcard selection:\n   845\t  `slots-match?` (`query.cljc:444-449`) treats only `_`, nil, and the\n   980\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   981\t| Rule              | Defect when                                                                                                                                       |\n   982\t+===================+===================================================================================================================================================+\n   983\t| `:tag`            | the element after the id is not a tag of §2.3                                                                                                     |\n   984\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   985\t| `:arity`          | the count differs from the tag's body arity plus one                                                                                              |\n   986\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   987\t| `:slot-kind`      | a slot's value is not of the slot's kind: a `node` slot that is not an address, a `nodes` slot that is not a vector of addresses, a `data` or     |\n   988\t|                   | `key` slot failing `plain-data?`, a `bool` slot that is not `true`/`false`                                                                        |\n   989\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   990\t| `:saturation`     | a saturated slot (§2.4) is nil                                                                                                                    |\n   991\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   992\t| `:id-resolves`    | a child id in a `node` or `nodes` slot names no row of the loaded set                                                                             |\n   993\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   994\t| `:acyclic`        | following child ids from a row revisits a row already on its own path                                                                             |\n   995\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   996\t| `:root-reachable` | a row of the loaded set is not reachable from the root row                                                                                        |\n   997\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   998\t| `:variable-bounds`| a `:variable` name must be bound by an enclosing `:lambda`'s params, else the frontend must have emitted `:global`; the defect names the path |\n   999\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1000\t\n  1001\tThe three reference rules are the row counterpart of §7.5's\n  1002\t`:target-bounds`: a row's child slots are references, and they must\n  1003\tresolve within the loaded set and close under the root, as a jump target\n  1004\tmust land inside its segment. Acyclicity is also implied by the per-row\n  1005\taddress check whenever it runs — a row's id commits to its children's\n  1006\tids, so a cycle would break the hash (§4.1) — and the rule stands\n  1007\tregardless, so that a load taking ids as given still names the defect.\n  1008\t\n  1009\tThere is no encoder-domain rule: §4.2 blocks identity uses outright rather\n  1010\tthan admitting a domain.\n  1011\t\n  1012\t### 7.5 Vector rules\n  1013\t\n  1014\tDefects name a pc. Rules 1–4 of `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.6 (one segment,\n  1015\tdense pcs, sorted by pc, refs resolve to entities) are satisfied by the\n  1016\tvector form by construction and have no rule here; rules 5 and 6 and the\n  1017\tUCF §7.3.4 checks are translated as follows.\n  1018\t\n  1019\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1020\t| Rule             | Defect when                                                                                                                                         |\n  1021\t+==================+=====================================================================================================================================================+\n  1022\t| `:nonempty`      | the vector has no instructions                                                                                                                      |\n  1023\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1024\t| `:mnemonic`      | the first element is not a mnemonic of `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`)                                                                         |\n  1025\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1026\t| `:arity`         | the tuple's count differs from the mnemonic's arity in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 as saturated by UCF §7.3.2                                         |\n  1027\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1028\t| `:operand-kind`  | an operand is not of its kind: `:const` and `:store-put` values or `:store-get`/`:store-put` keys fail `plain-data?` (the `key` kind of §2.2), a    |\n  1029\t|                  | `:var` index or `:closure` arity is not a non-negative integer, a `:global` name is not a symbol, a `:ffi-call` op or `:resume` parked id is not a keyword, a `:gensym`  |\n  1030\t|                  | prefix is not a string                                                                                                                              |\n  1031\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1032\t| `:saturation`    | a saturated operand (`:gensym` prefix, `:stream-make` buffer, `:call` tail?, `:ffi-call` argc) is nil                                               |\n  1033\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1034\t| `:target-bounds` | a `:jump`/`:branch-false` target or a `:closure` body is not an integer in `[0, length)`                                                            |\n  1035\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1718\t\n  1719\t### 9.1 The boundary, not a sweep\n  1720\t\n  1721\tThe walker stays on map ASTs exactly as built, retaining named variable evaluation. What changes is the\n  1722\tboundary around them — the projection in, the loader and the readers of\n  1723\trows behind it. The semantics do not change; the boundary does.\n  1724\t\n  1725\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1726\t| Site                                             | Change                                                                                                              |\n  1727\t+==================================================+=====================================================================================================================+\n  1728\t| walker, `ast_walker.cljc`                        | Retains named variable evaluation (`:name`). `:lambda` arm binds by name. Does not use De Bruijn numbering. Adds `:stream/close` arm (§3.2).                                                                                                      |\n  1729\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1730\t| loader, new                                      | validate rows (§7.4), reconstruct the map AST: `rows → map` (§6.1), the successor of `datoms->ast`                  |\n  1731\t|                                                  | (`v2.cljc:494-559`) with content addresses in place of allocated ids                                                |\n  1732\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1733\t| linearizer,                                      | reads the row relation through the same `get-attr`-over-an-index shape it has today (`lower-node`'s `(get-attr e    |\n  1734\t| `linearize.cljc:87-148`,                         | :yin/type)` becomes a lookup by row id); `ast-children` becomes a table lookup of `node`/`nodes` slot positions;    |\n  1735\t| `:230-242`                                       | `lower` takes a row set and returns a vector plus the §5.3 provenance table; emits `:var name`, `:global name`, and `:closure params body`; `lower-ast` (`:245-252`) goes away     |\n  1736\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1737\t| codec, `v2.cljc:372-559`                         | becomes `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`, the projection pair of §6.5 mapping Universal AST to Canonical Rows/Datoms.            |\n  1738\t|                                                  | `:yin/address` added; `:global` node added; retains `:yin/params` on lambda and `:yin/name` on variable |\n  1739\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1740\t| `code/mnemonics`, `code/well-formed?`,           | `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`) adds `:global`. `well-formed?` keeps judging datom batches on the projection path before projection; the shared vector validator of §7.5 runs on   |\n  1741\t| `code.cljc:12-180`                               | both paths after it                                                                                                 |\n  1742\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1743\t| `semantic/load-image`,                           | decodes from the vector directly on the primary path; positional frame binding for `:closure`; `:global` opcode resolves store → primitives → modules; decodes the new operands (`:var name`, `:global name`, `:closure params body`); both paths call the §7.5 validator   |\n  1744\t| `semantic.cljc:524-596`                          |                                                                                                                     |\n  1745\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1746\t| frontends                                        | keep emitting map ASTs to the stream (as named universal ASTs). They do not perform the tuple projection. |\n  1747\t| `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`,                    | All projection to Semantic Tuples and side-tables is deferred to the Encoder Observer (`yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`).        |\n  1748\t| `python.cljc`, `php.cljc`                        |                                                                                                                     |\n  1749\t|                                                  | never survive the boundary. The r7 adapter's ordering rules become the standing contract: first reject any `:macro? |\n  1750\t|                                                  | true` lambda that is not the value operand of a `yin/def` as `:stray-macro-lambda`, then turn each admitted one into|\n  1751\t|                                                  | a declaration at its tree index and path, then strip the flag                                                       |\n  1752\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1753\t| datom-batch adapter,                             | for media that carry datoms: derives `:yin/harvest` from entity order and entity identity while inlining, recording |\n  1754\t| standing                                         | every `[j path]` at which each definition entity is emitted (the `datoms->ast` recursion, `v2.cljc:494-559`,        |\n  1755\t|                                                  | extended to carry the path and the entity id, since today it tracks neither); one entity with several paths becomes |\n  1756\t|                                                  | one occurrence group; the adapter assigns no `:decl` values (§8.5)                                                  |\n  1757\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1758\t\n  1759\tThe v1 lineage (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/ast_walker_test.cljc`,\n  1760\t`test/yin/vm/ast_conversion_test.cljc`, `runtime_regression_test.cljc`) is\n  1761\toutside this design; it stays on its own map path until it is retired.\n  1762\t\n  1763\t### 9.2 The observer lane: an integration item to scope, not a solved detail\n  1764\t\n  1765\tProgram input reaches an evaluator through\n  1780\t  shell and the `yin.vm.stream-observer` tests before the loaders are\n  1781\t  switched.\n  1782\t- **The program-input predicate.** `engine/executable-program-datom?`\n  1783\t  (`engine.cljc:511-513`) decides what counts as program input by testing\n  1784\t  the attribute namespace of an `[e a v t m]` row, and\n  1785\t  `append-program-datoms` (`engine.cljc:590-612`) builds on it. A row\n  1786\t  batch has no attribute slot. A repository search on 2026-09-14 found no\n  1787\t  caller of the v2 `append-program-datoms` beyond its own overload, so a\n  1788\t  row counterpart is not presumed necessary; whether any live\n  1789\t  composition reaches the predicate is what the implementation phase\n  1790\t  establishes before deciding.\n  1791\t\n  1792\tBoth are integration items to scope in the implementation phase.\n  1793\t\n  1794\t### 9.3 What stays on datoms, and why\n  1795\t\n  1796\tThis is §1's datom-layer jobs\n  1797\tseen from the migration side:\n  1798\t\n  1799\t- **The ledger, every ref, and every ledger event** (§8): mutability,\n  1800\t  history, provenance, and custody are datom properties, and the\n  1801\t  transactor is what possesses them.\n  1802\t- **`dao.space.index` and published covered indexes**: they index datoms,\n  1803\t  and the scale path of §6.5 goes through them.\n  1804\t- **The macro expander's event log** (§8.4): events are entities with\n  1805\t  attributes, one per attempt; only their values changed kind.\n  1806\t- **The datom projection of code** (§6.5): kept for the three query classes\n  1807\t  it alone serves, regenerable from the content at any time.\n  1808\t- **Execution traces** (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.6): state over time is a\n  1809\t  datom stream by design and is untouched.\n  1810\t\n  1811\t---\n  1812\t\n  1813\t## §10 Acceptance blockers and open items\n  1814\t\n  1815\t1. **Type-preserving canonical encoding in `dao.jing`** — declared,\n  1816\t   inherited, and **blocking every identity and deduplication use**\n  1817\t   (§4.2). `order-normalize` (`jing.cljc:45-64`) coerces every sequential\n  1818\t   value to a vector and `pr-str` drops metadata, so lists, seqs, and\n  1819\t   metadata-bearing vectors collide with plain vectors. No restricted\n  1820\t   value domain is claimed; the round-2 list-rejection rule is withdrawn\n  1821\t   as unsound. Rows, vectors, ledger-record maps, row\n  1822\t   deduplication, DAG sharing, process-local caches, content resolution by\n  1823\t   address, and ledger rows are all blocked alike until `dao.jing`'s\n  1824\t   encoding passes the conformance pairs of §4.2. This design does not\n  1825\t   close the item.\n  1826\t2. **The store-key domain is the `data` domain** (§2.2): every validator,\n  1827\t   extraction query, and store-slice encoding must use that one\n  1828\t   definition. This document now states it uniformly; the implementation\n  1829\t   phase must not reintroduce a narrower one, and the corpus must contain\n  1830\t   a numeric key beside the keyword one.\n  1831\t3. **Complete occurrence identity** (§2.5, §5.3, §8.4) — design work\n  1832\t   with stated mechanisms, not yet implementable. Source origins\n  1833\t   `[:source medium batch j]` must be supplied to the lowering and\n  1834\t   recorded in instruction provenance and initial expansion events;\n  1835\t   nested expansion events must carry both the declared-ref parent link\n  1836\t   and the parent record address, and the record must be minted after\n  1837\t   the parent's. The medium identity coordinate is whatever the\n  1838\t   composition names its program medium by; this document does not fix\n  1839\t   its shape, and that shape must be fixed before an origin can be\n  1840\t   written. The expander incarnation (§8.4.1) is a composition-minted\n  1841\t   random token, never a log-allocated id or a stream position; its\n  1842\t   uniqueness is the composition's warranty, discharged by the token's\n  1843\t   randomness, and no transactor is claimed to provide it. The\n  1844\t   `:incarnation` field of `ctx` and the token-minting step at\n  1845\t   construction are additions to `yin.vm.macro.md`'s `ctx` and expander\n  1846\t   construction that this document cannot make there.\n  1847\t4. **Macro batch and admission preservation** (§8.5) — design work with\n  1848\t   stated mechanisms. The ordered batch, the admission-order harvest\n  1849\t   catalogue of occurrence groups, the derived macro-only declaration\n  1850\t   catalogue, its admission validation, the validate-before-strip rule,\n  1851\t   and the adapter's path-and-entity tracking during inlining must exist\n  1852\t   before any tuple program containing a macro definition can be\n  1853\t   admitted. The legacy datom-batch contract is\n  1854\t   preserved only through the adapter-derived catalogue; tuple-native\n  1855\t   frontends operate\n  1856\t   under the separate declaration-order contract stated there. The v2\n  1857\t   expander itself does not exist yet (`src/cljc/yin/vm/` has no\n  1858\t   `macro.cljc`) and is not a blocker for tuple evaluation without\n  1859\t   macros; §8.4's event shape is specified against `yin.vm.macro.md`, not\n  1860\t   against code.\n  1861\t5. **Conservative dependency completion** (§7.7.2, §7.7.3) — design\n  1862\t   work with stated mechanisms. Global name discharge requires searching the\n  1863\t   store slice by key or resolving via primitive/module profiles (§7.7.2);\n  1864\t   convergence is over work items and all dependency facts, with the\n  1865\t   positional-environment context abstraction for values. Neither analysis exists in code;\n  1866\t   until they do, an emitter may only report `:incomplete`.\n  1867\t6. **Effect normalization** (§7.7.1): the footprint table must be\n  1868\t   published with the execution-contract stamp, and the tree/segment\n  1869\t   requirement-set equality must be tested over the corpus. FFI ops are a\n  1870\t   receiver capability requirement, never a store-slice requirement.\n  1871\t7. **The lowering profile** (§5.2.1): `\"ast-v1\"` must be published as a\n  1872\t   pinned document before any derivation record is written; until then\n  1873\t   no derivation can be verified, only content integrity (§5.2.2).\n  1874\t8. **UCF §7.6.1 needs two amendments** (§7.7.2, §7.7.3): its\n  1875\t   any-carried-environment satisfaction sentence is unsound and must be\n  1876\t   replaced by the store-slice-by-key / profile rule of §7.7.2 (an environment never\n  1877\t   discharges a name), and its address-per-segment fixed\n  1878\t   point must become the work-item fixed point. This document cannot edit\n  1879\t   UCF.\n  1880\t9. **The observer-lane batch shape and the program-input predicate**\n  1881\t   (§9.2) — integration items with no design answer in this document. The\n  1882\t   §8.5 ordered batch is a candidate shape for the AST medium, not a\n  1883\t   verified one.\n  1884\t10. **Ledger event representation on the transactor** (§8.2): the\n  1885\t    event-entity form is legal under `local-datom?` as written, but the\n  1886\t    map-`m` provenance path (`transact.cljc:15-29`) and the metadata-op\n  1887\t    interpreter `dao.space.schema.datomic.md` §4 names are not exercised by\n  1888\t    this design's tests yet. The naming rows need neither; the provenance\n  1889\t    link from a naming fact to its event does.\n  1890\t11. **The primitive-profile registry** — inherited from UCF §7.11. Every\n  1891\t    retained name obligation is checked by profile under UCF §7.5.2, and\n  1892\t    §7.7.2 reads callable effects from profiles; until\n  1893\t    `yin.vm/primitives` (`v2.cljc:100-132`) is published with profiles,\n  1894\t    dependency closure reports names and `:incomplete`, not satisfiable\n  1895\t    bindings.\n  1896\t12. **Per-lambda subterm addressing** — dissolved by the flat-row form\n  1897\t    (§4.3): every node is addressed by construction, and the remaining\n  1898\t    one-segment-per-lambda lowering question is a §5.2.1 profile concern.\n  1899\t13. **The round-trip law** (§7.2, part 1) — a first-class conformance\n  1900\t    obligation, not a blocker: for every corpus program, `map → rows →\n  1901\t    map` is the identity on the canonical map AST and `rows → map →\n  1902\t    rows` is the identity on the rows, and both directions must be\n  1903\t    tested over the corpus before any row set is treated as canonical.\n  1904\t    The corpus already lives as map ASTs in the test tree, so the test\n  1905\t    is writable now.\n  1906\t14. **`yin.vm.semantic.md` Instruction Grammar Amendment** (§5.1) — the `ast-v1` lowering\n  1907\t    profile (§5.2.1) pins the §5.3 table of `yin.vm.semantic.md`, which is currently\n  1908\t    the name-based one. That document's §2.4 and §5.3 must be amended to reflect the\n  1909\t    Named variable instruction grammar\n  1910\t    and the positional binding rule of §7.7.2 (under-arity call → missing slots `nil`; extra arguments beyond `arity` are dropped)\n  1911\t    before any derivation record is written.\n   150\t\n   151\t`coverage` and `checkpoint` are the two operations that read both halves of a\n   152\tsession. They are index-side functions over a shape `dao.stream.observer`\n   153\tpublishes (`{:stream :cursor :ingress-gaps}`) — the dependency runs\n   154\t`dao.space.index → dao.stream.observer`, never the reverse — and the\n   155\tgeneric loop stays ignorant of every index field.\n   156\t\n   157\tDriven as `(run-on-stream {:observer o :consumer index-state} ready?\n   158\tindex/fold-batch index/flush-staged)` over any `dao.stream` reader\n   159\thandle attached through `dao.stream.observer/attach`. The coordination\n   160\tinspects no field of the index state; it drives it as readily as a VM —\n   161\twhich is the point of decision 2. **Publication is an explicit composition\n   162\tstep**, not a policy inside the loop: the composition calls `publish!`\n   163\tbetween rounds (after every *n* rounds, on `blocked`, on a timer — its\n   164\tchoice), and only the *resumption* of a staged publication lives inside\n   165\t`run`, so that `ready?` stays false until the intake has accepted every\n   166\tpayload. Calling `publish!` while a publication is still staged is a caller\n   167\terror (the composition checks `ready?` first); a staged publication is never\n   168\treplaced, only resumed (§4.1). `:ids` records whether it is `:owned` by this\n   169\tsession or `:shared` with others (§3.1); the distinction governs what a\n   170\tcheckpoint may restore (§4.2).\n   171\t\n   172\t### 2.2 `fold-batch`\n   173\t\n   174\tOne batch, one pure fold. **The fold is atomic per batch**: steps 0–2\n   175\tvalidate and plan against the *whole* normalized batch before step 3\n   176\ttouches a tree or step 4 advances `:ids`; a batch rejected at any step\n   177\tleaves all four trees, `:ids`, and `:max-t` unchanged, increments the\n   178\tmonotonic `:rejected` count, appends exactly one drainable defect event\n   179\t(batch ordinal, position, reason), and advances `:batch` by exactly one —\n   180\tnever zero, or every later cross-session ordinal (§3.2) would drift on the\n   181\tfirst malformed input even without a `gap`. `:rejected` is the durable\n   182\trecord that the index is partial; `:defects` is the diagnostic a driver\n   183\treads and clears. Draining never touches `:rejected`, so completeness\n   184\tsurvives a drain, a checkpoint, and a restore; a composition that needs the\n   185\t*identities* of omitted batches retains the drained events itself. An\n   186\t**empty** admitted batch is a valid no-op: it advances `:batch` once and\n   187\tchanges nothing else — not trees, `:ids`, `:max-t`, or `:rejected`.\n   188\t\n   189\t0. **Normalize the outer value.** `run-on-stream` hands `load` one stream\n   190\t   value, and media differ in what that is: a transactor appends one\n   191\t   transaction-record map per `append!`; a program medium appends a vector\n   192\t   of rows. The grammar, checked in this order: a map containing\n   193\t   `:dao.space/transaction` is one element; a vector of exactly five whose\n   194\t   first slot is an integer and second a keyword is one d5 element (a bare\n   195\t   row is never mistaken for a five-row batch, and a five-row batch's first\n   196\t   slot is a vector, not an integer); any other sequential is a batch of\n   197\t   elements, each normalized by the same two rules and nothing else (no\n   198\t   nesting); anything else is a whole-batch defect at position `nil`.\n   199\t1. **Admit elements.** Each element is a d5 row or a\n   200\t   `{:dao.space/transaction {:t :datoms}}` record, flattened. Admission is\n   201\t   *looser than* `datom/local-datom?`, which `element-datoms` applies today\n   202\t   and which requires a non-negative `e`: in `:unresolved` mode admission is\n   203\t   exactly `local-datom?` minus that one clause — integer `e` of either\n   204\t   sign, namespaced keyword `a`, `t ≥ 0`, integer `m`. `v` is unconstrained\n   205\t   by `local-datom?` already, so a negative ref-valued `v` needs no schema\n   206\t   knowledge to be *admitted*; the schema first matters at step 2. In\n   207\t   `:resolved` mode admission *is* `local-datom?`. Strictness is restored\n   208\t   after step 2: every row that reaches step 3 satisfies `local-datom?`. A\n   209\t   transaction-record element is admitted by the same per-datom rule applied\n   210\t   to each of its datoms plus the record's own shape checks (one shared `t`,\n   211\t   non-empty). Anything not admitted is a defect for the whole batch — the\n   212\t   session continues; a throwing `load` would leave the cursor on the bad\n   213\t   batch forever, the head-of-line hazard `yin.vm.macro.md` decision 11\n   214\t   removes. Bad input is data; a bug in the fold itself is the throw.\n   215\t   `datoms-from-elements` keeps its strict, throwing contract for the\n   216\t   one-shot path; the fold uses an admitting, non-throwing variant of the\n   217\t   same per-element rule.\n   218\t2. **Validate the mode and resolve identity** (§3). Identity lives in three\n   219\t   slots — `e`, a `v` under an attribute `:schema` declares a ref, and `m`,\n   220\t   the metadata *reference* — and the transactor already resolves all three\n   221\t   (`dao.space.transact` collects `m` tempids explicitly). The index does the\n   222\t   same; §3.1's matrix says what each slot may hold in each mode, and any\n   223\t   violation is a whole-batch defect. In `:unresolved` mode every tempid in\n   224\t   the batch — as `e`, as a declared-ref `v`, as `m`, or only as one of the\n   225\t   latter two (a reference to an entity the batch never describes) — is\n   226\t   mapped through a batch-local table to a fresh durable id from `:ids`, in\n   227\t   **first-occurrence order** — rows in normalized-batch order, and within a\n   228\t   row `e`, then declared-ref `v`, then `m` — so allocation is identical on\n   229\t   every host, and the mapping is emitted as resolution facts. Reserved ids\n   230\t   (§3.1: `0 ≤ id < datom/first-user-id`, where `default-op` lives) are never\n   231\t   allocated; whether a reserved id is admitted in a given slot is the\n   232\t   matrix's rule, not a blanket pass. `:resolved` mode allocates nothing.\n   233\t3. **Fold.** Rows and resolution facts are `dao.data.btree/conj`ed into the\n   234\t   four trees — a persistent insert that shares every unmodified node with\n   235\t   the previous state. A node created by the insert carries no address mark;\n   236\t   dirtiness *is* that absence, a property of the node and of nothing else\n   237\t   (`btree.cljc` `node-store` skips every child slot that already carries an\n   238\t   address), so §4.1's `store-tree` later emits exactly what this and\n   239\t   subsequent folds created. Covered indexes are sets; a duplicate row is a\n   240\t   no-op (`conj` returns the same set). `:max-t` becomes the greater of\n   241\t   itself and the batch's greatest row `t`; it is `nil` until the first row\n   242\t   is folded, and an empty batch leaves it as it was.\n   243\t4. **Advance** `:batch`, and `:ids` in `:unresolved` mode.\n   244\t\n   245\tThe `:indexes` after batch *n* are a pure function of (index state before,\n   246\tbatch), on every host.\n   247\t\n   248\t### 2.3 What the two views mean over each medium\n   249\t\n   250\t`dao.space.query/current` and `history` are unchanged: they are\n   251\tinterpreters over rows, and the index state's trees are a row source.\n   252\t\n   253\t| Medium | `t` in rows | `current` | `history` |\n   254\t|---|---|---|---|\n   255\t| transactor-written (records) | allocated by the writer, monotonic | greatest-`t`-wins per `[e a v]`, retractions removed — full Datomic semantics | the exact rows; `history` promises no ordering — over a `db-value` the rows come in EAVT-walk order, not writer order — so order by `t` explicitly when it matters |\n   256\t| a medium whose rows carry no writer-allocated `t` (all rows `t 0`, `m default-op`) | not meaningful | the *set* of facts asserted — correct for a medium of assertions with no retractions | degenerate: all rows share `t 0`; order is the observer's batch ordinal, available through resolution facts (§3), **not** through `t` |\n   257\t\n   258\tThe observer does not paper over the second row by minting `t` — decision\n   259\t4. A composition that wants transaction-time semantics over such a medium\n   260\troutes it through a transactor (the writer allocates `t`); one that wants\n   285\ttempids allocated *per batch* (from `(- datom/first-user-id)` downward, by\n   286\tconvention; the observer relies only on their being negative). Two\n   287\tconsecutive batches both contain `-16`. Folding them raw into one index\n   288\twould merge unrelated entities — the silent-wrong-data case.\n   289\t`dao.space.transact` resolves tempids within one transaction; the observer\n   290\tmust do the same per batch, and only the observer can, because only it\n   291\tholds the cross-batch allocator.\n   292\t\n   293\t**Why a mode, fixed at construction.** Durable ids are positive integers on\n   294\tone number line. A medium whose writer supplies positive ids — a\n   295\ttransactor's log, where ids are the caller's free choice (`transactor.cljc`\n   296\ttakes `:db/id` as given and derives a watermark over `t` only) — and an\n   297\tobserver minting positive ids for tempids cannot share an index without\n   298\teventually colliding, and no watermark over `t` says anything about `e`. An\n   299\texplicit integer partition (writer even, observer odd, say) would avoid\n   300\truntime coordination, but only by changing the writer's allocation\n   301\tcontract, which an arbitrary existing writer has not agreed to; two modes\n   302\tare the simpler default and the one this note specifies. So an index state\n   303\tis either `:resolved` — it indexes media whose ids are already durable and\n   304\tallocates nothing — or `:unresolved` — it indexes media that carry only\n   305\ttempids and owns every positive user id in its index, allocating from\n   306\t`datom/first-user-id` upward.\n   307\t\n   308\t**The mode matrix.** Identity-bearing slots are `e`, a declared-ref `v`,\n   309\tand `m`. Ids partition into three disjoint classes: **tempid** `id < 0`;\n   310\t**reserved** `0 ≤ id < datom/first-user-id` (`default-op` lives there);\n   311\t**user-positive** `id ≥ datom/first-user-id`.\n   312\t\n   313\t| Slot | `:resolved` | `:unresolved` |\n   314\t|---|---|---|\n   315\t| `e` | reserved or user-positive pass through — exactly what `local-datom?` and the transactor admit today, so Phase 0′ parity is unconditional; tempid → defect | tempid → allocated; reserved or user-positive → defect (an unexplained positive reference: the writer cannot know the observer's ids) |\n   316\t| declared-ref `v` | reserved or user-positive pass; tempid → defect (this is the schema-aware check `local-datom?` alone does not make) | tempid → allocated; reserved passes; user-positive → defect |\n   317\t| `m` | reserved or user-positive pass; tempid → defect | reserved passes; tempid → allocated; user-positive → defect |\n   318\t| undeclared `v` | any value, untouched | any value, untouched |\n   319\t\n   320\tReserved `e` on a `:resolved` medium is *admitted*, not enforced against:\n   321\t`datom.md`'s reservation has never been enforced by the runtime, and an\n   322\tindex that started enforcing it would silently diverge from every existing\n   323\tread path. If enforcement is ever wanted it is a separate, stated decision.\n   324\t\n   325\tA composition that needs both kinds of medium in one query opens two index\n   326\tstates as two sources; `dao.space.query` keeps sources as separate\n   327\tdb-values and joins only where the query says so. **Equating `?e` across\n   328\tindependently allocated sources is never meaningful** — not across a\n   329\t`:resolved` and an `:unresolved` source, not across two `:unresolved`\n   330\tsessions with separate allocators, not across two `:resolved` media with\n   331\tdifferent writers — because unification compares integers and carries no\n   332\tallocation-domain check. This is advisory in the query (nothing can enforce\n   333\tit there) and therefore a contract on compositions: joins across sources go\n   334\tthrough values. Phase 2's shared allocator is a *composition-owned value\n   335\tthreaded serially* through the `:unresolved` sessions that share it — never\n   336\tan atom the sessions share — and only such sessions may equate `?e`. A\n   337\tsession records whether its `:ids` is `:owned` or `:shared`; the\n   338\tconsequence for recovery is §4.2's.\n   339\t\n   340\t### 3.2 Resolution facts\n   341\t\n   342\tFor every tempid `τ` the observer maps to durable `δ` in batch *n* —\n   343\twhichever slot it appeared in — it asserts, in its own attribute namespace:\n   344\t\n   345\t```\n   346\t[δ :dao.space.index/batch   n   n default-op]\n   347\t[δ :dao.space.index/tempid  τ   n default-op]\n   348\t```\n   349\t\n   350\tThe row's `t` is the observer's batch ordinal *n* — its own logical clock,\n   351\tnever a host clock, and not the observed rows' `t`. `m` is\n   352\t`datom/default-op`: the attribute namespace alone distinguishes these facts\n   353\tfrom observed rows, and nothing else is needed. That is a **contract on\n   354\tmedia, not a check in the index**: a medium must not assert attributes in\n   355\t`:dao.space.index/*`. The index does not inspect attribute namespaces\n   356\t(decision 6), so a medium that violates the reservation shares `[e a v]`\n   357\tkeys with resolution facts and owns the resulting `current` semantics\n   358\t(greatest-`t` shadowing, or the `m`-conflict throw on a same-`t` meet).\n   359\tWith the reservation honoured, resolution facts and observed rows have\n   360\tdisjoint `[e a v]` keys and can neither shadow nor conflict. These are\n   361\tfacts *about* observation, distinct from the observed rows. They make\n   362\t\"which batch asserted this entity\" — `[?e :dao.space.index/batch ?n]` — a\n   363\tquery, and they are the raw material for cross-medium provenance.\n   364\t\n   365\t**Refs are batch-local; cross-medium coordinates are values.** Refs are\n   366\tresolved only for attributes the supplied `:schema` declares as\n   367\t`:db.type/ref` — exactly as the transactor relocates only declared refs\n   368\ttoday — and a declared ref *always* resolves within the batch it appears\n   369\tin. There is no way, at attribute granularity, to say \"this `-16` names an\n   370\tentity on another medium\": the index would allocate a batch-local entity\n   371\tfor it, and a shared allocator only prevents *collisions*, it establishes\n   372\tno *correspondence*. So a coordinate into another medium must be carried as\n   373\ta **value**, qualified by the batch it refers to, under an attribute the\n   374\tschema does not declare a ref; the join is then value-based over\n   375\tresolution facts — `[?e :dao.space.index/batch t'] [?e\n   376\t:dao.space.index/tempid -16]` — and is the composition's, since only the\n   377\tcomposition knows which observer's ordinal `t'` corresponds to the\n   378\tproducer's batch number. That correspondence is a constant offset **only\n   379\twhile neither session has seen a `gap`**; a gap count records that gaps\n   380\toccurred, not how many batches were lost, so after one the offset must be\n   381\tre-derived or provenance across it marked unknown (open question 2).\n   382\t\n   383\tThis has a consequence for `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1, recorded here as a\n   384\t**required amendment** to that document: its `:yin/source-call` is written\n   385\tas both an external coordinate (initial expansion: an eid in the source\n   386\tbatch) and a log-local ref (nested expansion: a node in the log copy). One\n   387\tattribute cannot be both. It must split — an undeclared value attribute for\n   388\tthe external coordinate, qualified by `:yin/source-batch` as it already is,\n   389\tand a declared-ref attribute for the log-local node — so that\n   390\t`event-schema` declares only the latter a ref and the index resolves it\n   391\tbatch-locally while leaving the former as the value the join needs. The\n   392\tschema is the composition's to supply (for a program medium,\n   393\t`yin.vm/schema`; for an expander's log, the amended `event-schema`); the\n   394\tindex learns which attributes are refs from it and nothing else. An\n   395\tundeclared attribute holding a negative number is a value, not a ref, and is\n   396\tleft alone.\n   397\t\n   398\t### 3.3 What is not promised\n   399\t\n   400\tDurable ids are the *observer's* reading. Two independent observers over the\n   401\tsame medium allocate independently; their ids agree only if they are the\n   402\tsame session or share a checkpoint (§4.2). This is the same statement as\n   403\t`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2's \"refs across media are meaningful only for durable\n   404\tids\", now with the owner of \"durable\" named: it is whichever dao.space\n   405\tobserver indexed the medium.\n   406\t\n   407\t---\n   408\t\n   409\t## 4. Publication and the checkpoint\n   410\t\n   700\tincluding the within-row `e`, `v`, `m` order; a `gap` immediately before\n   701\t`checkpoint` yields a candidate carrying the recovery cursor and the\n   702\tincremented gap count; `:max-t` `nil` on an empty stream and the reopened\n   703\tempty checkpoint deriving watermark `0`; `run-on-stream` `blocked`/`end` behaviour;\n   704\tthe publication state machine — `ok/full/full/ok`, refusal at the manifest,\n   705\ta terminal outcome after accepted blobs with `:next` preserved in the\n   706\tthrow, a successful resumption followed by a read failure, and a second\n   707\t`publish!` after more batches appending only the blobs stored since the\n   708\tfirst (count them); the **checkpoint candidate** round-trips through\n   709\t`pr-str`/`read-string` (it, unlike a live session, is plain data), a\n   710\tcandidate whose manifest is not yet materialized is *not* promoted, nor\n   711\tone whose manifest is present but one leaf is absent (the walk must check\n   712\tevery address, not only the manifest); `restore` refuses a mismatched mode\n   713\tor schema and refuses a `:shared` allocator (a sibling session allocated\n   714\tafter the candidate was captured); the F2 hazard pinned\n   715\tdeterministically both ways through the btree's `:test` ref seam (`:test`\n   716\tref-type plus `clear-test-refs!`): a resumed session restored through a\n   717\t`:test`-ref storage, published, drained, refs cleared → the next query\n   718\t*throws* \"missing index segment\"; the same sequence through the\n   719\tsession-constructed `:strong` storage → no throw; and, gated to the JVM\n   720\twhere the read path is not already `:strong`, a session restored through\n   721\t`restored-indexes` carries `bt/default-ref-type*`.\n   722\t\n   723\t**Phase 1 — a medium with batch-local tempids (composition test).** The\n   724\tindex is exercised over a medium another observer also reads: two\n   725\t`dao.stream.observer` sessions on one `program-out`, one driving\n   726\t`ast-walker`, one driving `dao.space.index` in `:unresolved` mode. The\n   727\tindex code under test knows nothing of the VM; the test does. Tests:\n   728\tnegative `e` rows are admitted and resolved, positive `e` on this medium is\n   729\ta defect; the VM's value and the\n   730\tindex's `current` view from the same batches; after each observer round —\n   731\tthe test drives the indexer, then queries; appending drives nothing — `q`\n   732\tover `db-value` sees every batch folded up to the cursor and a `db-value`\n   733\ttaken before the round is unchanged; two batches with overlapping\n   734\ttempids index as distinct entities with correct resolution facts;\n   735\t`[?e :yin/type :lambda]` finds every lambda the program contained; a ref\n   736\tattribute resolved, an undeclared negative value untouched; `history`\n   737\tdocumented as degenerate over `t 0` rows.\n   738\t\n   739\t**Phase 2 — cross-medium provenance.** Prerequisite: the `yin.vm.macro.md`\n   740\t§4.1 amendment (§3.2) splitting `:yin/source-call` into a value attribute\n   741\tfor external coordinates and a ref attribute for log-local nodes. Observers\n   742\tover `program-in`, `program-out`, and the macro log, the `:unresolved`\n   743\tsessions sharing one composition-owned allocator threaded serially; the\n   744\toffset fact between the expander's `:t` and the observer's ordinal. Tests:\n   745\tan initial expansion's external coordinate joins to the source entity\n   746\tthrough resolution facts by value; a nested expansion's log-local ref\n   747\tresolves within the log batch; the same negative number on two media names\n   748\ttwo entities; a `v`-only and an `m`-only tempid each get an id and facts;\n   749\tthe `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 chain source-call → event → expansion-root\n   750\tanswered by `dao.space.query/q` over the three indexed media; after a\n   751\tforced `gap` on one session the offset is reported void.\n   752\t\n   753\t**Phase 3 — checkpoint validity and recovery.** Over a file-backed medium\n   754\t(`dao.stream.file`): capture a candidate, verify it against the durable\n   755\tstore, restart from it, fold the suffix, and assert the resumed index\n   756\tequals a from-origin index and the watermark equals `0` when nothing was\n   757\tfolded, else `max-t + 1` over checkpoint plus suffix; a candidate whose\n   758\tmanifest was never materialized is not promoted and restart uses the\n   759\tprevious checkpoint; a `:shared` candidate is not promoted; restored\n   760\t`:ingress-gaps` and `:rejected` survive; a same-logical-stream in-process reopen\n   761\trestores, folds the suffix, and only then enables writes. Over a\n   762\tmemory-log, assert the documented behaviour instead: a resumed session\n   763\tstarts fresh at `:oldest` of the new log and the checkpoint is not\n   764\tconsulted. The transactor's `create!` derives its watermark from a\n   765\tcheckpoint plus suffix when one is offered.\n   766\t\n   767\t---\n   768\t\n   769\t## 8. Open questions\n   770\t\n   771\t- **One session per medium, or one over a merged view?** Per medium keeps\n   772\t  each cursor honest and makes `gap` attributable; a merged view needs a\n   773\t  merge interpreter that `dao.stream.md` does not (and should not) provide.\n   774\t  This note leans per-medium with a shared allocator, but the allocator\n   775\t  then couples the sessions — a composition choice to state when it is\n   776\t  made.\n   777\t- **Retention and `gap`.** Over an evicting transport the observer inherits\n   778\t  `dao.stream.observer`'s gap accounting; an index with a gap is a partial\n   779\t  index and must say so, and a cross-session ordinal offset (§3.2) is void\n   780\t  after one. Whether a published manifest should carry the gap count is\n   781\t  undecided.\n   782\t- **Where the checkpoint lives** (§4.2).\n   783\t- **Recovery of a shared allocation domain.** `restore` refuses a\n   784\t  `:shared` `:ids` (§4.2); a composition that shares an allocator across\n   785\t  `:unresolved` sessions needs a checkpoint over all of them at once and a\n   786\t  replay discipline that reassigns the same ids in the same order. Deferred\n   787\t  with Phase 2's topology choice.\n   788\t- **Whether `snapshot` is retired** in favour of \"run a throwaway session to\n   789\t  `blocked` and take its trees\". Probably, but `snapshot`'s status\n   790\t  vocabulary (`:ended`/`:blocked`/`:gap`/`:defect`) should survive as the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 3 ':var|:closure|variable|De Bruijn|debruijn|arity|params|environment' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | head -n 240 && rg -n -C 2 'ast->semantic-bytecode|semantic-bytecode' src test docs/design | head -n 240\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 3 ':var|:closure|variable|De Bruijn|debruijn|arity|params|environment' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | head -n 240 && rg -n -C 2 'ast->semantic-bytecode|semantic-bytecode' src test docs/design | head -n 240\"","aggregated_output":"35-\n36-**Code and State are Datoms.** Instructions are entities with attributes.\n37-Execution state is plain data: the control is `{:segment id :pc n}`, the\n38:environment a map, the operand stack a vector, the continuation a vector of\n39-frames each `{:pc :env :stack-base :segment}`. The optional trace vocabulary\n40-(§3.6) deposits exactly these as `[e a v t m]` with `t` the step counter,\n41-the \"endgame\" of `cesk-space-optimization.md` §6: execute fast, emit the\n--\n133-| `:yin.code/source`  | ref (AST node eid)       | every lowered instruction; absent when hand-assembled |\n134-+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\n135-\n136:Linearity is therefore an **explicit fact**, `:yin.code/pc`, not the vector\n137-order of the batch and not entity-id arithmetic. Entity ids are tempids that\n138-a transactor may remap; vector order is a transport accident. The\n139-well-formedness rule (§2.6) requires the batch to be *sorted* by pc so a\n--\n157-+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n158-| `:const`                | `:yin.code/value v`                           | `val ← v`                                                                        |\n159-+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n160:| `:var`                  | `:yin.code/name sym`                          | `val ← resolve(E, S, prims, modules, sym)`                                       |\n161-+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n162:| `:closure`              | `:yin.code/params [..]`, `:yin.code/body ref` | `val ← {:type :closure :params .. :entry pc(body) :segment seg :env E}`          |\n163-+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n164-| `:push`                 | —                                             | `St ← St ⧺ [val]`                                                                |\n165-+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n--\n209-`:literal :load-var :lambda :call :return :branch :jump :gensym :store-get\n210-:store-put :stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close\n211-:park :resume :current-cont :tailcall :dao.stream.apply/call`. The loader\n212:maps mnemonics onto that table (`:const`→`:literal`, `:var`→`:load-var`,\n213:`:closure`→`:lambda`, `:branch-false`→`:branch`, `:ffi-call`→\n214-`:dao.stream.apply/call`, `:call`+`:tail? true`→`:tailcall`) and the table\n215-gains `:push 22` and `:halt 23`. `:move` stays unused. Keeping the datom\n216-mnemonics semantic and the image integers mechanical is the point: a query\n--\n221-A literal lives inline in the `v` slot of `:yin.code/value`, polymorphic\n222-exactly as `:yin/value` is today (numbers, strings, keywords, vectors, maps,\n223-sets, nil). Host functions never appear in code datoms; a primitive is named\n224:by symbol in `:var` and resolved at run time through the supplied\n225-`:primitives`, which is how the walker already avoids putting host objects in\n226-the program. A shared or large constant pool (`:yin.code/const-ref` to a\n227-constant entity) is reserved and not needed for the current corpus.\n--\n246-\n247-```\n248-seg   :yin.code/type :segment   :yin.code/length 14\n249:0  :closure   params [x]  body →6\n250-1  :push\n251-2  :const     10\n252-3  :push\n253-4  :call      argc 1  tail? false\n254-5  :halt\n255:6  :var       +               ; body of the lambda, out of line\n256-7  :push\n257:8  :var       x\n258-9  :push\n259-10 :const     1\n260-11 :push\n--\n427-  `val`, and the operand stack `St` (a vector; `St` is control, not store,\n428-  because it is part of what the machine is *doing*, and it is saved in\n429-  frames).\n430:- **E (environment)**: a persistent map `sym → value`, extended on closure\n431:  entry with `merge closure-env (zipmap params args)`, as the walker does.\n432-  Lexical addressing is reserved (§6.4).\n433-- **S (store)**: the map `key → value` holding `yin/def` results, stream\n434-  handles, cursor entries, and the FFI pair — unchanged from `engine`.\n--\n445-$$\\begin{aligned}\n446-&\\text{const}:&& \\langle seg,pc,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,I.v,\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n447-&\\text{var}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\rho(E,S,I.name),\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n448:&\\text{closure}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\mathrm{clo}(I.params,I.entry,seg,E),\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n449-&\\text{push}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,val,\\,St \\Vert [val],E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n450-&\\text{jump}:&& \\to \\langle seg,\\,I.target,\\,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n451-&\\text{branch-false}:&& \\to \\langle seg,\\,(val\\ ?\\ pc{+}1 : I.target),\\,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n--\n559-+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n560-| `:literal v`                                            | `const v`                                                          |\n561-+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n562:| `:variable s`                                           | `var s`                                                            |\n563-+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n564-| `:lambda ps body`                                       | `closure ps →L` ; body emitted later at label L ending in `return` |\n565-+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n--\n585-+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n586-\n587-Evaluation order is the walker's (operator, then operands left to right),\n588:so effect order is identical, which the parity suite checks. Tail position\n589-is read from `:yin/tail?` as yang already marks it; `:if` branches inherit\n590-it; nothing new is inferred. Labels are resolved to instruction refs in a\n591-second pass; the linearizer emits refs, never pc integers.\n--\n661-\n662-Reserved, additive, none required for Phase 1–3:\n663-\n664:- **Lexical addressing**: `:var` gains `:yin.code/depth`/`:yin.code/index`\n665-  when the linearizer proves the name lexically bound; frames become vectors;\n666-  only unbound names walk store → primitives → modules.\n667-- **Superinstructions**: `:push-const`, `:push-var` fused by the loader (not\n--\n740-\n741-The semantic VM meets the performance line on the JVM, so `yin.repl.core`\n742-now defaults to `:semantic`; `(vm :ast-walker)` still selects the walker.\n743:Behavioural parity for the switch rests on the Phase 1–3 suites and on a\n744-REPL corpus that produced identical output under both evaluators. The corpus\n745-covered arithmetic, `def`/`defn`, `*1` history, 10⁵ tail calls, 2·10⁴-deep\n746-non-tail recursion, streams, Python and PHP input, datom literals, and error\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-774-**The Dedicated AST Indexer:** This row relation (called `$ast` in queries) is maintained by the dedicated AST indexer (§1). The indexer is a `dao.stream` observer peer to the evaluators (§7.1); its input is the row batches of §7.1; its output is this row relation plus the occurrence relation below; it is distinct from `dao.space.index`, which indexes datoms only (§6.5); it is a projection keeper per §1's Query layer, so discarding it loses nothing.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-775-\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:776:`map → rows` is the **codec boundary projection** (`yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`): strip the §2.5\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-777-exclusions, saturate per §2.4, positionalize per the §2.3 dictionary,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-778-merkle per §4.1. It is the standing contract of the Encoder Observer, not a\n--\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-1735-| `:230-242`                                       | `lower` takes a row set and returns a vector plus the §5.3 provenance table; emits `:var name`, `:global name`, and `:closure params body`; `lower-ast` (`:245-252`) goes away     |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-1736-+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1737:| codec, `v2.cljc:372-559`                         | becomes `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`, the projection pair of §6.5 mapping Universal AST to Canonical Rows/Datoms.            |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-1738-|                                                  | `:yin/address` added; `:global` node added; retains `:yin/params` on lambda and `:yin/name` on variable |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-1739-+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n--\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-1745-+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-1746-| frontends                                        | keep emitting map ASTs to the stream (as named universal ASTs). They do not perform the tuple projection. |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1747:| `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`,                    | All projection to Semantic Tuples and side-tables is deferred to the Encoder Observer (`yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`).        |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-1748-| `python.cljc`, `php.cljc`                        |                                                                                                                     |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md-1749-|                                                  | never survive the boundary. The r7 adapter's ordering rules become the standing contract: first reject any `:macro? |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,290p' src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc && sed -n '1,235p' src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc && rg -n 'defn.*ast->datoms|defn.*datoms->ast|:variable|:lambda' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc | head -n 180\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,290p' src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc && sed -n '1,235p' src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc && rg -n 'defn.*ast->datoms|defn.*datoms->ast|:variable|:lambda' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc | head -n 180\"","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.space.transactor\n  \"The agent-side transactor: a plain value with explicit named operations\n   over an explicit local dao.stream handle plus an explicit intake pool\n   of dao.stream writers (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Publication from an\n   agent; docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md).\n\n   A transactor is not a stream in v2 — it is an interpreter over one\n   (dao.stream.md, Composition): append!/transact! allocate transaction time\n   and write transaction records through the local stream's writer surface,\n   and reads go through the caller's own local handle, which the transactor\n   never owns.\n\n   Spec (create!):\n\n     {:local-stream s   ; must satisfy stream/reader? and stream/writer?;\n                        ; supplied, never created, registered, or closed\n      :intake-pool [p]  ; non-empty; every member satisfies stream/writer?;\n                        ; supplied, never created, registered, or closed\n      :name n}          ; optional, diagnostic only\n\n   create! validates surfaces, never retention: stream/reader? and\n   stream/writer? do not establish the complete retention the history scan\n   requires. The host composition supplies a handle created by\n   dao.stream.memory-log/create!; supplying an evicting transport is a\n   host-assembly defect (detectable, deliberately not checked — T18,\n   docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md).\n\n   The transactor owns transaction time. Every append! or transact! writes\n   exactly ONE atomic transaction record to the local stream\n\n     {:dao.space/transaction {:t <nonnegative-integer> :datoms [...]}}\n\n   through exactly one local append, so no reader can ever observe a torn\n   transaction. t is allocated from a per-value watermark: on create! the\n   retained history is read from its origin (index/snapshot-datoms) and the\n   next t is derived as 0 for an empty history or 1 + the maximum integer\n   datom t otherwise. A malformed history throws. The scan is the causality\n   boundary; a caller-supplied next-t is never accepted.\n\n   Operational outcomes are data; argument defects keep throwing. The\n   watermark advances if and only if the local append answered\n   :dao.stream/ok, so retrying the identical call re-attempts the identical\n   t. publish! builds the covered indexes over the local stream and\n   enqueues them into the intake pool (dao.space.index/publish-index!).\n   Publication is explicit and acknowledges enqueuing only, never observer\n   materialization.\n\n   Closing is per value: it rejects further appends/transacts with\n   {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed} but neither closes nor erases\n   the local stream or the intake pool.\"\n  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n            [dao.space.index :as index]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]))\n\n\n(defn- entity->datoms\n  \"One datom per k/v pair, all sharing transaction time `t`. `:db/id` is\n   required: a durable log cannot mint per-batch tempids without colliding\n   across appends. Attribute order within one entity's own datoms is\n   whatever the host's map iteration yields — unspecified across platforms.\"\n  [m t]\n  (when-not (contains? m :db/id)\n    (throw (ex-info \"entity map requires :db/id\" {:entity m})))\n  (let [e (:db/id m)]\n    (mapv (fn [[a v]]\n            (let [d [e a v t datom/default-op]]\n              (when-not (datom/local-datom? d)\n                (throw (ex-info \"invalid persistent local datom\"\n                                {:datom d, :entity m})))\n              d))\n          (remove (fn [[a _v]] (= a :db/id)) m))))\n\n\n(defn- pad-datom\n  \"Pad [e a v] / [e a v t m] to the canonical 5-tuple. The transactor owns\n   transaction time: a non-nil explicit t is rejected, a nil t is stamped\n   with the allocated `t`. A nil m takes the default assertion op; an\n   explicit m (0, a retraction) is preserved.\"\n  [d t]\n  (let [[e a v dt dm] d]\n    (when-not (nil? dt)\n      (throw\n        (ex-info\n          \"the transactor owns transaction time: explicit datom t is rejected\"\n          {:datom d})))\n    (let [padded [e a v t (if (nil? dm) datom/default-op dm)]]\n      (when-not (datom/local-datom? padded)\n        (throw (ex-info \"invalid persistent local datom\"\n                        {:datom padded, :input d})))\n      padded)))\n\n\n(defn- val->datoms\n  [val t]\n  (cond\n    (map? val) (entity->datoms val t)\n    (and (vector? val) (<= 3 (count val) 5)) [(pad-datom val t)]\n    :else\n    (throw\n      (ex-info\n        \"a value must be an entity map or datom vector [e a v] / [e a v nil m]\"\n        {:val val}))))\n\n\n#_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\n\n\n(defn- with-write-lock\n  \"Serialize timestamp allocation and append on hosts with shared-memory\n   threads. ClojureScript and ClojureDart calls are synchronous within one\n   isolate, so the direct call has the same single-writer semantics.\"\n  [lock f]\n  #?(:clj (locking lock (f))\n     :default (f)))\n\n\n(defn- derive-next-t\n  \"Derive the next transaction time from a flattened retained history: 0 for\n   an empty history, else 1 + the maximum datom t. A datom t that is not a\n   non-negative integer is a malformed history and throws — this scan is the\n   causality boundary of the value.\"\n  [datoms]\n  (if (empty? datoms)\n    0\n    (let [ts (mapv index/datom-t datoms)]\n      (when-not (every? #(and (integer? %) (<= 0 %)) ts)\n        (throw\n          (ex-info\n            \"malformed retained history: datom t must be a non-negative integer\"\n            {:history datoms})))\n      (inc (reduce max ts)))))\n\n\n(defn- append-packet!\n  \"Append one atomic transaction record to the local stream. The answer is\n   validated with the contract's own validator before it is interpreted:\n   :dao.stream/ok advances the watermark and returns the v2 receipt; any\n   other conforming outcome is returned as data with the watermark\n   unchanged, so the same t is still pending and retrying the identical\n   call re-attempts it; a non-outcome answer is folded to\n   :dao.stream/transport-error with the raw answer retained under\n   :dao.stream/answer.\"\n  [local-stream next-t t datoms]\n  (let [record {:dao.space/transaction {:t t, :datoms datoms}}\n        answer (stream/append! local-stream record)]\n    (if-not (stream/valid-outcome? :append! answer)\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n       :dao.stream/answer answer}\n      (do (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome answer))\n            (swap! next-t inc))\n          (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome answer))\n            {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n             :dao.space/t t\n             :dao.space/datoms datoms}\n            answer)))))\n\n\n(defn create!\n  \"Create a transactor value from a spec — the value, or a throw: the\n   failure modes are a malformed spec and a malformed retained history,\n   both of which a caller can only abort on.\n\n   Validates surfaces, never retention (D2/T18): stream/reader? and\n   stream/writer? check the dao.stream reader and writer surfaces, and\n   neither establishes the complete-retention transport the history scan\n   requires. The host composition supplies a handle created by\n   dao.stream.memory-log/create!; supplying an evicting transport is a\n   host-assembly defect — detectable, deliberately not checked, because a\n   type check would couple dao.space to memory-log by name and reject a\n   future correct complete-retention transport.\"\n  [spec]\n  (when-not (map? spec)\n    (throw\n      (ex-info\n        \"transactor spec must be a map\"\n        {:spec spec})))\n  (let [{:keys [local-stream intake-pool name]} spec]\n    (when (contains? spec :next-t)\n      (throw\n        (ex-info\n          \"the transactor derives transaction time from retained history; :next-t is not accepted\"\n          {:spec spec})))\n    (when-not (and (stream/reader? local-stream)\n                   (stream/writer? local-stream))\n      (throw\n        (ex-info\n          \"transactor spec requires a :local-stream satisfying the dao.stream reader and writer surfaces\"\n          {:spec spec})))\n    (when-not (and (coll? intake-pool) (seq intake-pool))\n      (throw\n        (ex-info\n          \"transactor spec requires a non-empty :intake-pool of streams satisfying the dao.stream writer surface\"\n          {:spec spec})))\n    (doseq [intake intake-pool]\n      (when-not (stream/writer? intake)\n        (throw\n          (ex-info\n            \"transactor :intake-pool members must satisfy IDaoStreamWriter\"\n            {:intake-pool intake-pool}))))\n    (let [t (derive-next-t (index/snapshot-datoms local-stream))]\n      {:dao.space/transactor true\n       :local-stream local-stream\n       :intake-pool intake-pool\n       :name (or name \"transactor\")\n       :next-t (atom t)\n       :state (atom {:closed false})})))\n\n\n(defn append!\n  \"Append one entity map or datom vector as a single atomic transaction.\n   Answers the v2 receipt on ok — {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   :dao.space/t t :dao.space/datoms datoms} — the local append's conforming\n   outcome as data otherwise (watermark unchanged, same t still pending),\n   and {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed} on a closed transactor.\n   A malformed value still throws: it is a defect in the caller's own\n   argument, detected before any stream is touched.\"\n  [log val]\n  (let [{:keys [local-stream next-t state]} log]\n    (with-write-lock\n      next-t\n      (fn []\n        (if (:closed @state)\n          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}\n          (let [t @next-t\n                datoms (val->datoms val t)]\n            (when (empty? datoms)\n              (throw (ex-info \"append! produced no datoms\" {:val val})))\n            (append-packet! local-stream next-t t datoms)))))))\n\n\n(defn transact!\n  \"Commits a non-empty collection of entity maps or datom vectors as a\n  single atomic transaction: every datom shares one allocated t and lands\n  in one transaction record through exactly one local append, so no partial\n  prefix is possible. Answers the v2 receipt on ok, the local append's\n  conforming outcome as data otherwise, and\n  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed} on a closed transactor. Throws\n  when the collection is empty, any item is invalid, or the expansion\n  yields no datoms.\"\n  [log tx-data]\n  (when (empty? tx-data)\n    (throw (ex-info \"transact! requires at least one transaction item\"\n                    {:tx-data tx-data})))\n  (let [{:keys [local-stream next-t state]} log]\n    (with-write-lock\n      next-t\n      (fn []\n        (if (:closed @state)\n          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}\n          (let [t @next-t\n                datoms (into [] (mapcat #(val->datoms % t)) tx-data)]\n            (when (empty? datoms)\n              (throw (ex-info \"transact! produced no datoms\" {:tx-data tx-data})))\n            (append-packet! local-stream next-t t datoms)))))))\n\n\n(defn close!\n  \"Close this transactor value: further append!/transact! answer\n   {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed} rather than throwing. Per\n   value — neither the local stream nor the intake pool is closed or\n   erased. Idempotent; linearizes after an in-flight append.\"\n  [log]\n  (let [{:keys [next-t state]} log]\n    (with-write-lock\n      next-t\n      (fn []\n        (swap! state assoc :closed true)\n        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))))\n\n\n(defn publish!\n  \"Build and enqueue the covered indexes over the local stream into the\n   intake pool (dao.space.index/publish-index!), returning its\n   {:manifest-address ... :manifest ...}. opts are passed through to\n   publish-index! (e.g. :branching-factor, :select-stream). Publication is\n   explicit and acknowledges enqueuing only, never observer\n   materialization.\"\n  ([log] (publish! log nil))\n  ([log opts]\n   (index/publish-index! (:local-stream log) (:intake-pool log) opts)))\n(ns dao.space.transact\n  \"Stateless transaction processing library. Extracts tx-data into raw datoms\n   by parsing entity maps, resolving tempids, enforcing uniqueness, and\n   generating cardinality-one retractions using dao.space.query.\"\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.datom :as datom]\n            [dao.space.query :as query]))\n\n\n(defn- tempid?\n  [x]\n  (or (and (string? x) (str/starts-with? x \"tid_\")) (and (number? x) (neg? x))))\n\n\n(defn- parse-op\n  [m e a v default-op]\n  (if (map? m)\n    (let [{:keys [db/op], :as without-op} (dissoc m :db/op)\n          op-kw (or op default-op)\n          datom-m-val (get datom/reserved op-kw op-kw)]\n      {:op op-kw,\n       :e e,\n       :a a,\n       :v v,\n       :m-raw datom-m-val,\n       :m-map (when (seq without-op) without-op)})\n    (let [op-kw (if (= m (:db/retract datom/reserved)) :db/retract :db/add)\n          m-raw (or m (:db/assert datom/reserved))]\n      {:op op-kw, :e e, :a a, :v v, :m-raw m-raw, :m-map nil})))\n\n\n(defn- map->datoms\n  \"Convert an entity map into a seq of [e a v] or parsed ops.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [e (or (:db/id m) (str \"tid_\" (gensym)))]\n    (mapcat (fn [[a v]]\n              (if (and (or (sequential? v) (set? v))\n                       (not (map? v))\n                       (not= a :db/valueType)\n                       (not= a :db/cardinality))\n                (map #(parse-op nil e a % :db/add) v)\n                [(parse-op nil e a v :db/add)]))\n            (dissoc m :db/id))))\n\n\n(defn- parse-tx-data\n  \"Normalize tx-data into a flat sequence of parsed ops.\"\n  [tx-data]\n  (mapcat (fn [item]\n            (if (map? item)\n              (map->datoms item)\n              (let [[op e a v m] item] [(parse-op m e a v op)])))\n          tx-data))\n\n\n(defn- lookup-ident-eid\n  \"Resolve an ident keyword to its entity id.\"\n  [base-datoms same-tx-ident->eid ident]\n  (or (get same-tx-ident->eid ident)\n      (let [res (query/match (query/current (query/relation base-datoms))\n                  ['_ :db/ident ident])]\n        (when (seq res) (nth (first res) 0)))))\n\n\n(defn- effective-ref-attrs-for-tx\n  \"Compute the set of :db.type/ref attributes, incorporating same-tx schema changes.\"\n  [base-datoms parsed-ops same-tx-ident->eid]\n  (let [eid->ident (into {}\n                         (keep (fn [d]\n                                 (when (= (nth d 1) :db/ident)\n                                   [(nth d 0) (nth d 2)])))\n                         base-datoms)\n        eid->ident (into eid->ident\n                         (keep (fn [{:keys [op e a v]}]\n                                 (when (and (= op :db/add) (= a :db/ident))\n                                   [e v])))\n                         parsed-ops)\n        existing-refs (into #{}\n                            (keep (fn [d] (get eid->ident (nth d 0))))\n                            (query/match (query/current (query/relation\n                                                          base-datoms))\n                              ['_ :db/valueType :db.type/ref]))\n        gaining (into #{}\n                      (keep (fn [{:keys [op e a v]}]\n                              (when (and (= op :db/add)\n                                         (= a :db/valueType)\n                                         (= v :db.type/ref))\n                                (let [resolved-e (if (keyword? e)\n                                                   (lookup-ident-eid\n                                                     base-datoms\n                                                     same-tx-ident->eid\n                                                     e)\n                                                   e)]\n                                  (or (when (keyword? e) e)\n                                      (get eid->ident resolved-e))))))\n                      parsed-ops)\n        losing (into #{}\n                     (keep (fn [{:keys [op e a v]}]\n                             (when (and (= op :db/retract)\n                                        (= a :db/valueType)\n                                        (= v :db.type/ref))\n                               (let [resolved-e (if (keyword? e)\n                                                  (lookup-ident-eid\n                                                    base-datoms\n                                                    same-tx-ident->eid\n                                                    e)\n                                                  e)]\n                                 (or (when (keyword? e) e)\n                                     (get eid->ident resolved-e))))))\n                     parsed-ops)]\n    (-> existing-refs\n        (into gaining)\n        (#(apply disj % losing)))))\n\n\n(defn- expand-m-map\n  \"Expand inline metadata maps into additional datoms.\"\n  [op eid t]\n  (let [m-map (:m-map op)]\n    (if m-map\n      (let [m-datoms (mapcat\n                       (fn [[a v]]\n                         (if (and (or (sequential? v) (set? v)) (not (map? v)))\n                           (map #(vector eid a % t (:db/assert datom/reserved))\n                                v)\n                           [[eid a v t (:db/assert datom/reserved)]]))\n                       m-map)\n            op' (assoc op :m-raw eid)]\n        [op' m-datoms (inc eid)])\n      [op [] eid])))\n\n\n(defn- collect-tempids\n  \"Collect all tempids from parsed ops and extra datoms.\"\n  [ops effective-ref-attrs extra-datoms]\n  (let [op-tempids (mapcat (fn [{:keys [e a v m-raw]}]\n                             (cond-> []\n                               (tempid? e) (conj e)\n                               (and (contains? effective-ref-attrs a)\n                                    (tempid? v))\n                               (conj v)\n                               (tempid? m-raw) (conj m-raw)))\n                           ops)\n        extra-tempids (mapcat (fn [d]\n                                (cond-> []\n                                  (tempid? (nth d 0)) (conj (nth d 0))\n                                  (and (contains? effective-ref-attrs (nth d 1))\n                                       (tempid? (nth d 2)))\n                                  (conj (nth d 2))\n                                  (tempid? (nth d 4)) (conj (nth d 4))))\n                              extra-datoms)]\n    (into #{} (concat op-tempids extra-tempids))))\n\n\n(defn- resolve-tempids\n  \"Assign sequential permanent entity IDs to tempids.\"\n  [tempids start-eid]\n  (let [sorted-tempids (sort (fn [a b]\n                               (cond (and (number? a) (number? b)) (compare a b)\n                                     (and (string? a) (string? b)) (compare a b)\n                                     (number? a) -1\n                                     :else 1))\n                             tempids)]\n    (zipmap sorted-tempids\n            (range start-eid (+ start-eid (count sorted-tempids))))))\n\n\n(defn- apply-tempid-map\n  \"Replace tempids with permanent entity IDs in parsed ops.\"\n  [ops tempid-map effective-ref-attrs]\n  (mapv (fn [op]\n          (let [e' (if (tempid? (:e op))\n                     (get tempid-map (:e op) (:e op))\n                     (:e op))\n                v' (if (and (contains? effective-ref-attrs (:a op))\n                            (tempid? (:v op)))\n                     (get tempid-map (:v op) (:v op))\n                     (:v op))\n                m-raw' (if (tempid? (:m-raw op))\n                         (get tempid-map (:m-raw op) (:m-raw op))\n                         (:m-raw op))]\n            (assoc op\n                   :e e'\n                   :v v'\n                   :m-raw m-raw')))\n        ops))\n\n\n(defn prepare-tx\n  \"Takes base-datoms and tx-data.\n   Returns {:datoms [...] :tempids {...} :next-eid new-eid :next-t new-t}.\n   Can optionally pass next-eid and next-t to avoid scanning.\"\n  [{:keys [base-datoms tx-data next-t next-eid]}]\n  (let [t (or next-t\n              (inc (reduce (fn [mx d] (max mx (nth d 3))) 0 base-datoms)))\n        base-eid (or next-eid\n                     (reduce (fn [mx d]\n                               (let [e (nth d 0)\n                                     v (nth d 2)]\n                                 (-> mx\n                                     (max (if (number? e) (inc e) 0))\n                                     (max (if (and (number? v)\n                                                   (not= (nth d 1) :db/ident))\n                                            (inc v)\n                                            0)))))\n                             datom/first-user-id\n                             base-datoms))\n        parsed-ops (parse-tx-data tx-data)\n        same-tx-ident->eid\n        (into {}\n              (keep (fn [{:keys [op e a v]}]\n                      (when (and (= op :db/add) (= a :db/ident)) [v e])))\n              parsed-ops)\n        effective-ref-attrs\n        (effective-ref-attrs-for-tx base-datoms parsed-ops same-tx-ident->eid)\n        alloc-base-eid (reduce\n                         (fn [eid {:keys [op e a v m-raw]}]\n                           (let [mx (if (and (= op :db/add)\n                                             (number? e)\n                                             (>= e datom/first-user-id))\n                                      (max eid (inc e))\n                                      eid)\n                                 mx (if (and (= op :db/add)\n                                             (contains? effective-ref-attrs a)\n                                             (number? v)\n                                             (>= v datom/first-user-id))\n                                      (max mx (inc v))\n                                      mx)\n                                 mx (if (and (= op :db/add)\n                                             (number? m-raw)\n                                             (>= m-raw datom/first-user-id))\n                                      (max mx (inc m-raw))\n                                      mx)]\n                             mx))\n                         base-eid\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:21:   `yin.vm.macro.md`). The `:lambda` arm ignores `:macro?`; there is no macro\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:361:        :variable\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:364:        :lambda (let [{:keys [params body]} node]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:602:               :variable (let [v (engine/resolve-var env\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:608:               :lambda (recur nil\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:93:             :variable (emit! e :var :yin.code/name (get-attr e :yin/name))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:94:             :lambda (let [l (fresh!)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:235:    :lambda [(:body node)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:221:   :lambda 4,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:257:                    :lambda 4,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:298:   :yin/type {},      ; keyword (:literal, :variable, :lambda, ...)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:314:   ;; :variable\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:372:(defn ast->datoms-with-root\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:416:                  :variable (do (emit! e :yin/type :variable)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:418:                  :lambda (do (emit! e :yin/type :lambda)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:485:(defn ast->datoms\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:494:(defn datoms->ast\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:509:       :variable (assoc base :name (get-attr root-id :yin/name))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:510:       :lambda (let [body-eid (get-attr root-id :yin/body)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '145,180p;420,465p;550,575p;655,670p' && nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '415,430p;820,838p;1010,1030p;1178,1220p;1738,1746p;1898,1912p' && nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md | sed -n '175,248p;340,405p;665,690p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | sed -n '145,180p;420,465p;550,575p;655,670p' && nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '415,430p;820,838p;1010,1030p;1178,1220p;1738,1746p;1898,1912p' && nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md | sed -n '175,248p;340,405p;665,690p'\"","aggregated_output":"   145\t(\"this branch goes to that instruction\"), survives id remapping when both\n   146\tends are remapped together, and is checkable.\n   147\t\n   148\t### 2.4 The core semantic primitives\n   149\t\n   150\tMachine model: an **accumulator machine with an operand stack** (§4). `val`\n   151\tis the accumulator; `St` the operand stack; `K` the continuation of return\n   152\tframes. This is the stack-class formulation `streams-all-the-way-down.md` §3\n   153\tsays survives the log-structured reading; a register model would not.\n   154\t\n   155\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   156\t| `:yin.code/op`          | Operand attributes                            | Effect on the machine                                                            |\n   157\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   158\t| `:const`                | `:yin.code/value v`                           | `val ← v`                                                                        |\n   159\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   160\t| `:var`                  | `:yin.code/name sym`                          | `val ← resolve(E, S, prims, modules, sym)`                                       |\n   161\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   162\t| `:closure`              | `:yin.code/params [..]`, `:yin.code/body ref` | `val ← {:type :closure :params .. :entry pc(body) :segment seg :env E}`          |\n   163\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   164\t| `:push`                 | —                                             | `St ← St ⧺ [val]`                                                                |\n   165\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   166\t| `:call`                 | `:yin.code/argc n`, `:yin.code/tail? bool`    | pop `n` args and the operator below them; apply (§4.3)                           |\n   167\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   168\t| `:return`               | —                                             | pop frame from `K`; restore `pc`, `E`, stack base; `val` unchanged; empty `K`    |\n   169\t|                         |                                               | halts                                                                            |\n   170\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   171\t| `:jump`                 | `:yin.code/target ref`                        | `pc ← target`                                                                    |\n   172\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   173\t| `:branch-false`         | `:yin.code/target ref`                        | `pc ← val ? pc+1 : target`                                                       |\n   174\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   175\t| `:halt`                 | —                                             | halt with `val` as the result                                                    |\n   176\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   177\t| `:gensym`               | `:yin.code/prefix`                            | `val ← fresh id`; id counter advances                                            |\n   178\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   179\t| `:store-get`            | `:yin.code/key`                               | `val ← S[key]`                                                                   |\n   180\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   420\t\n   421\t### 4.1 Configuration\n   422\t\n   423\t$$\\langle C, E, S, K \\rangle \\quad\\text{where}\\quad\n   424\tC = \\langle seg, pc, val, St \\rangle$$\n   425\t\n   426\t- **C (control)**: the segment id, the program counter, the accumulator\n   427\t  `val`, and the operand stack `St` (a vector; `St` is control, not store,\n   428\t  because it is part of what the machine is *doing*, and it is saved in\n   429\t  frames).\n   430\t- **E (environment)**: a persistent map `sym → value`, extended on closure\n   431\t  entry with `merge closure-env (zipmap params args)`, as the walker does.\n   432\t  Lexical addressing is reserved (§6.4).\n   433\t- **S (store)**: the map `key → value` holding `yin/def` results, stream\n   434\t  handles, cursor entries, and the FFI pair — unchanged from `engine`.\n   435\t- **K (continuation)**: a vector of frames, innermost last. Frame kinds:\n   436\t  `{:type :return :segment :pc :env :stack-base}` for calls, and the\n   437\t  effect-continuation frames the engine already defines\n   438\t  (`:dao.stream.apply/eval-call`, `:request-sent`), extended with\n   439\t  `:segment :pc :stack`.\n   440\t\n   441\tLet `I = code[seg][pc]` be the decoded instruction and `I.x` its operands.\n   442\t\n   443\t### 4.2 Transitions for the core instructions\n   444\t\n   445\t$$\\begin{aligned}\n   446\t&\\text{const}:&& \\langle seg,pc,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,I.v,\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   447\t&\\text{var}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\rho(E,S,I.name),\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   448\t&\\text{closure}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,\\mathrm{clo}(I.params,I.entry,seg,E),\\,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   449\t&\\text{push}:&& \\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,val,\\,St \\Vert [val],E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   450\t&\\text{jump}:&& \\to \\langle seg,\\,I.target,\\,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   451\t&\\text{branch-false}:&& \\to \\langle seg,\\,(val\\ ?\\ pc{+}1 : I.target),\\,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   452\t&\\text{halt}:&& \\to \\text{halted},\\ \\text{result} = val\n   453\t\\end{aligned}$$\n   454\t\n   455\twhere $\\rho$ is `engine/resolve-var` (env → store → primitives → modules).\n   456\t\n   457\t**call** with $n = I.argc$, $St = St' \\Vert [f, a_1..a_n]$:\n   458\t\n   459\t$$\\begin{aligned}\n   460\t&f = \\mathrm{clo}(ps, e, seg', E_c),\\ \\neg tail:&&\n   461\t\\to \\langle seg',\\,e,\\,val,\\,St',\\,E_c[ps \\mapsto a],\\,S,\\,K \\Vert [\\mathrm{ret}(seg,pc{+}1,E,|St'|)]\\rangle\\\\\n   462\t&f = \\mathrm{clo}(ps, e, seg', E_c),\\ tail:&&\n   463\t\\to \\langle seg',\\,e,\\,val,\\,St',\\,E_c[ps \\mapsto a],\\,S,\\,K\\rangle\\\\\n   464\t&f\\ \\text{primitive},\\ f(a)\\ \\text{a value } v:&&\n   465\t\\to \\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\,v,\\,St',E,S,K\\rangle\\\\\n   550\tdefined load-time condition.\n   551\t\n   552\t### 5.3 The flattening\n   553\t\n   554\tRecursive descent over the AST in evaluation order, emitting instructions\n   555\tinto a growing vector and lambda bodies out of line:\n   556\t\n   557\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   558\t| AST node                                                | Emitted sequence                                                   |\n   559\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   560\t| `:literal v`                                            | `const v`                                                          |\n   561\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   562\t| `:variable s`                                           | `var s`                                                            |\n   563\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   564\t| `:lambda ps body`                                       | `closure ps →L` ; body emitted later at label L ending in `return` |\n   565\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   566\t| `:application op args` (tail? τ)                        | `⟦op⟧ push ⟦a₁⟧ push … ⟦aₙ⟧ push call n τ`                         |\n   567\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   568\t| `:if t c a`                                             | `⟦t⟧ branch-false →A ⟦c⟧ jump →E A: ⟦a⟧ E:`                        |\n   569\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   570\t| `:dao.stream.apply/call op args`                        | `⟦a₁⟧ push … ⟦aₙ⟧ push ffi-call op n`                              |\n   571\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   572\t| `:stream/put target val`                                | `⟦target⟧ push ⟦val⟧ stream-put`                                   |\n   573\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   574\t| `:stream/cursor s`, `:stream/next s`, `:stream/close s` | `⟦s⟧ stream-cursor` etc.                                           |\n   575\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   655\tVM is only the stored form (five datoms per instruction rather than a few\n   656\tbytes) and the load step that decodes it. Execution cost is therefore\n   657\tbytecode-class by construction, and queryability is not paid for on the hot\n   658\tpath at all — it is paid once at load. The historic 1.0–1.1x register/stack\n   659\tline in `cesk-space-optimization.md` is the target; the walker sits at\n   660\t1.32x on that workload, the old graph-walking semantic VM at 1.69x.\n   661\t\n   662\tReserved, additive, none required for Phase 1–3:\n   663\t\n   664\t- **Lexical addressing**: `:var` gains `:yin.code/depth`/`:yin.code/index`\n   665\t  when the linearizer proves the name lexically bound; frames become vectors;\n   666\t  only unbound names walk store → primitives → modules.\n   667\t- **Superinstructions**: `:push-const`, `:push-var` fused by the loader (not\n   668\t  the linearizer, so datoms stay minimal).\n   669\t- **Host-array operand stack** inside the hot loop, materialized to a vector\n   670\t  only at park points (\"immutability observable only at park points\",\n   415\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   416\t| Side table             | Row                                                                                                                    | Keeper               |\n   417\t+========================+========================================================================================================================+======================+\n   418\t| source positions       | `[[:source medium batch j] root-address path file line col]`                                                           | the frontend that    |\n   419\t|                        |                                                                                                                        | parsed the file      |\n   420\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   421\t| frontend metadata      | `[origin root-address path key value]`                                                                                 | the frontend         |\n   422\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   423\t| variable names         | `[origin root-address path i name]`: the `i`-th parameter of the `:lambda` at `path` was written `name`; a `:variable` row's display name is the join through its binding lambda's path and index | the frontend         |\n   424\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   425\t| instruction provenance | `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]`                                                                        | the lowering (§5.3)  |\n   426\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   427\t| macro declarations     | `[[:source medium batch j] path]`: the definition at `path` of the batch's `j`-th tree is a macro definition; inside   | the frontend,        |\n   428\t|                        | the batch envelope this is written `[j path :yin.macro/definition]` (§8.5)                                             | consumed by the      |\n   429\t|                        |                                                                                                                        | expander             |\n   430\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   820\tthe pattern (`query.cljc:419-436`), and on the general path unifies every\n   821\trow through `unify-slots` (`query.cljc:799-809`), which first requires\n   822\t`tuple-shape-matches?` (`query.cljc:439-441`) and then unifies slot by\n   823\tslot. A final `& ?tail` binds the remaining slots as a vector and `& _`\n   824\tignores them (`query.cljc:431-436`, `:807-808`). The 3-slot EAV fast path\n   825\t(`query.cljc:899`) engages only when the source carries a fact index, which\n   826\t`fact-relation` (`query.cljc:172-177`) and the datom views supply; the code\n   827\trelations never do, so every code pattern takes the general path by\n   828\tconstruction.\n   829\t\n   830\tVerified shapes, each of which is a rule a query author may rely on:\n   831\t\n   832\t- **Per-tag selection.** `[?id :variable ?index]` matches only arity-3 rows.\n   833\t- **Joins across arities.** `[?site :application ?op _ _] [?op :global\n   834\t  ?f]` joins an arity-5 row to an arity-3 row on the address.\n   835\t- **Operand membership** goes through a predicate: `[(member? ?operands\n   836\t  ?x)]` with `member?` supplied under `:fns`; predicate arguments must\n   837\t  already be bound.\n   838\t- **Ledger as-of** (§8.6) and **ref-to-code joins** (§6.4) run in one\n  1010\tthan admitting a domain.\n  1011\t\n  1012\t### 7.5 Vector rules\n  1013\t\n  1014\tDefects name a pc. Rules 1–4 of `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.6 (one segment,\n  1015\tdense pcs, sorted by pc, refs resolve to entities) are satisfied by the\n  1016\tvector form by construction and have no rule here; rules 5 and 6 and the\n  1017\tUCF §7.3.4 checks are translated as follows.\n  1018\t\n  1019\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1020\t| Rule             | Defect when                                                                                                                                         |\n  1021\t+==================+=====================================================================================================================================================+\n  1022\t| `:nonempty`      | the vector has no instructions                                                                                                                      |\n  1023\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1024\t| `:mnemonic`      | the first element is not a mnemonic of `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`)                                                                         |\n  1025\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1026\t| `:arity`         | the tuple's count differs from the mnemonic's arity in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 as saturated by UCF §7.3.2                                         |\n  1027\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1028\t| `:operand-kind`  | an operand is not of its kind: `:const` and `:store-put` values or `:store-get`/`:store-put` keys fail `plain-data?` (the `key` kind of §2.2), a    |\n  1029\t|                  | `:var` index or `:closure` arity is not a non-negative integer, a `:global` name is not a symbol, a `:ffi-call` op or `:resume` parked id is not a keyword, a `:gensym`  |\n  1030\t|                  | prefix is not a string                                                                                                                              |\n  1178\texplicit `#{}`, never an absence. The conformance obligation: for every\n  1179\tcorpus tree, the requirement set computed from the tree and the one\n  1180\tcomputed from its lowered segment are **equal** in every field of\n  1181\t`:yin.k/requires`. A tag or mnemonic outside the table is\n  1182\t`:yin.k/undecodable`, the same outcome the validators give it.\n  1183\t\n  1184\t#### 7.7.2 Name obligations are resolved per store slice, and completion is conservative\n  1185\t\n  1186\tA name extracted from code (a `:global` row) is an **obligation**. Because this design preserves named variables across the Semantic Tuple boundary, the evaluator simply looks up the name in the environment.\n  1187\t\n  1188\t**Variables never fall through to globals.** An under-arity call leaves missing parameter names bound to `nil`; an over-arity call drops extra arguments beyond the params list length. An unbound variable name evaluates to `nil`; it never falls through to the store, primitives, or modules (since `:variable` and `:global` are distinct nodes). Therefore, name obligations are strictly and statically the set of `:global` names, never `:variable` names.\n  1189\t\n  1190\tEvery retained obligation is a required primitive or module export, checked by\n  1191\tprofile (UCF §7.5.2), and the **effects of a callable are read from its\n  1192\tprofile** (`:yin.k/effects`), never inferred from the name: `yin/def`\n  1193\tcontributes `:vm/store-put` and `require` contributes `:module/require`\n  1194\tbecause their profiles say so. Where a retained name has no profile at the\n  1195\temitter, discovery is `:incomplete`; conservatively retaining every\n  1196\tpossible primitive and module requirement is always admissible and never\n  1197\tmakes a `:complete` result wrong.\n  1198\t\n  1199\tThis rule is the amendment UCF §7.6.1's *Names* paragraph needs: its\n  1200\tsentence that a name \"is satisfied if it is bound in any environment the\n  1201\tvalue carries\" is the unsound test, and this document states the\n  1202\tcorrect rule in its place; UCF must be amended to say the same\n  1203\t(§10.8).\n  1204\t\n  1205\t#### 7.7.3 The fixed point converges over work items, not addresses\n  1206\t\n  1207\tThe unit of analysis is a **work item** `[code-address context]`, not an\n  1208\taddress. Two closures sharing one segment address with different captured\n  1209\tenvironments are two work items, because each enters the segment with its\n  1210\town environment (`semantic.cljc:199-205`) and so reaches different values (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), which\n  1211\tcontribute different work items; the name obligations of the segment are the same for both. The **context** of a work item is a finite abstraction: the\n  1212\tclosure's captured positional environment, and nothing else. This context\n  1213\tcontributes values (such as closures or streams bound within it), not\n  1214\tname discharge. Because values are finite, the set of contexts is finite\n  1215\tand the iteration terminates.\n  1216\t\n  1217\t- **Code once, contexts each.** Code is fetched, validated, and projected\n  1218\t  once per address; every newly discovered `[address context]` pair is\n  1219\t  analyzed, whether or not the address was seen before.\n  1220\t- **Parked ids.** Each extracted parked id must name a record in the\n  1738\t|                                                  | `:yin/address` added; `:global` node added; retains `:yin/params` on lambda and `:yin/name` on variable |\n  1739\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1740\t| `code/mnemonics`, `code/well-formed?`,           | `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`) adds `:global`. `well-formed?` keeps judging datom batches on the projection path before projection; the shared vector validator of §7.5 runs on   |\n  1741\t| `code.cljc:12-180`                               | both paths after it                                                                                                 |\n  1742\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1743\t| `semantic/load-image`,                           | decodes from the vector directly on the primary path; positional frame binding for `:closure`; `:global` opcode resolves store → primitives → modules; decodes the new operands (`:var name`, `:global name`, `:closure params body`); both paths call the §7.5 validator   |\n  1744\t| `semantic.cljc:524-596`                          |                                                                                                                     |\n  1745\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1746\t| frontends                                        | keep emitting map ASTs to the stream (as named universal ASTs). They do not perform the tuple projection. |\n  1898\t    one-segment-per-lambda lowering question is a §5.2.1 profile concern.\n  1899\t13. **The round-trip law** (§7.2, part 1) — a first-class conformance\n  1900\t    obligation, not a blocker: for every corpus program, `map → rows →\n  1901\t    map` is the identity on the canonical map AST and `rows → map →\n  1902\t    rows` is the identity on the rows, and both directions must be\n  1903\t    tested over the corpus before any row set is treated as canonical.\n  1904\t    The corpus already lives as map ASTs in the test tree, so the test\n  1905\t    is writable now.\n  1906\t14. **`yin.vm.semantic.md` Instruction Grammar Amendment** (§5.1) — the `ast-v1` lowering\n  1907\t    profile (§5.2.1) pins the §5.3 table of `yin.vm.semantic.md`, which is currently\n  1908\t    the name-based one. That document's §2.4 and §5.3 must be amended to reflect the\n  1909\t    Named variable instruction grammar\n  1910\t    and the positional binding rule of §7.7.2 (under-arity call → missing slots `nil`; extra arguments beyond `arity` are dropped)\n  1911\t    before any derivation record is written.\n   175\tvalidate and plan against the *whole* normalized batch before step 3\n   176\ttouches a tree or step 4 advances `:ids`; a batch rejected at any step\n   177\tleaves all four trees, `:ids`, and `:max-t` unchanged, increments the\n   178\tmonotonic `:rejected` count, appends exactly one drainable defect event\n   179\t(batch ordinal, position, reason), and advances `:batch` by exactly one —\n   180\tnever zero, or every later cross-session ordinal (§3.2) would drift on the\n   181\tfirst malformed input even without a `gap`. `:rejected` is the durable\n   182\trecord that the index is partial; `:defects` is the diagnostic a driver\n   183\treads and clears. Draining never touches `:rejected`, so completeness\n   184\tsurvives a drain, a checkpoint, and a restore; a composition that needs the\n   185\t*identities* of omitted batches retains the drained events itself. An\n   186\t**empty** admitted batch is a valid no-op: it advances `:batch` once and\n   187\tchanges nothing else — not trees, `:ids`, `:max-t`, or `:rejected`.\n   188\t\n   189\t0. **Normalize the outer value.** `run-on-stream` hands `load` one stream\n   190\t   value, and media differ in what that is: a transactor appends one\n   191\t   transaction-record map per `append!`; a program medium appends a vector\n   192\t   of rows. The grammar, checked in this order: a map containing\n   193\t   `:dao.space/transaction` is one element; a vector of exactly five whose\n   194\t   first slot is an integer and second a keyword is one d5 element (a bare\n   195\t   row is never mistaken for a five-row batch, and a five-row batch's first\n   196\t   slot is a vector, not an integer); any other sequential is a batch of\n   197\t   elements, each normalized by the same two rules and nothing else (no\n   198\t   nesting); anything else is a whole-batch defect at position `nil`.\n   199\t1. **Admit elements.** Each element is a d5 row or a\n   200\t   `{:dao.space/transaction {:t :datoms}}` record, flattened. Admission is\n   201\t   *looser than* `datom/local-datom?`, which `element-datoms` applies today\n   202\t   and which requires a non-negative `e`: in `:unresolved` mode admission is\n   203\t   exactly `local-datom?` minus that one clause — integer `e` of either\n   204\t   sign, namespaced keyword `a`, `t ≥ 0`, integer `m`. `v` is unconstrained\n   205\t   by `local-datom?` already, so a negative ref-valued `v` needs no schema\n   206\t   knowledge to be *admitted*; the schema first matters at step 2. In\n   207\t   `:resolved` mode admission *is* `local-datom?`. Strictness is restored\n   208\t   after step 2: every row that reaches step 3 satisfies `local-datom?`. A\n   209\t   transaction-record element is admitted by the same per-datom rule applied\n   210\t   to each of its datoms plus the record's own shape checks (one shared `t`,\n   211\t   non-empty). Anything not admitted is a defect for the whole batch — the\n   212\t   session continues; a throwing `load` would leave the cursor on the bad\n   213\t   batch forever, the head-of-line hazard `yin.vm.macro.md` decision 11\n   214\t   removes. Bad input is data; a bug in the fold itself is the throw.\n   215\t   `datoms-from-elements` keeps its strict, throwing contract for the\n   216\t   one-shot path; the fold uses an admitting, non-throwing variant of the\n   217\t   same per-element rule.\n   218\t2. **Validate the mode and resolve identity** (§3). Identity lives in three\n   219\t   slots — `e`, a `v` under an attribute `:schema` declares a ref, and `m`,\n   220\t   the metadata *reference* — and the transactor already resolves all three\n   221\t   (`dao.space.transact` collects `m` tempids explicitly). The index does the\n   222\t   same; §3.1's matrix says what each slot may hold in each mode, and any\n   223\t   violation is a whole-batch defect. In `:unresolved` mode every tempid in\n   224\t   the batch — as `e`, as a declared-ref `v`, as `m`, or only as one of the\n   225\t   latter two (a reference to an entity the batch never describes) — is\n   226\t   mapped through a batch-local table to a fresh durable id from `:ids`, in\n   227\t   **first-occurrence order** — rows in normalized-batch order, and within a\n   228\t   row `e`, then declared-ref `v`, then `m` — so allocation is identical on\n   229\t   every host, and the mapping is emitted as resolution facts. Reserved ids\n   230\t   (§3.1: `0 ≤ id < datom/first-user-id`, where `default-op` lives) are never\n   231\t   allocated; whether a reserved id is admitted in a given slot is the\n   232\t   matrix's rule, not a blanket pass. `:resolved` mode allocates nothing.\n   233\t3. **Fold.** Rows and resolution facts are `dao.data.btree/conj`ed into the\n   234\t   four trees — a persistent insert that shares every unmodified node with\n   235\t   the previous state. A node created by the insert carries no address mark;\n   236\t   dirtiness *is* that absence, a property of the node and of nothing else\n   237\t   (`btree.cljc` `node-store` skips every child slot that already carries an\n   238\t   address), so §4.1's `store-tree` later emits exactly what this and\n   239\t   subsequent folds created. Covered indexes are sets; a duplicate row is a\n   240\t   no-op (`conj` returns the same set). `:max-t` becomes the greater of\n   241\t   itself and the batch's greatest row `t`; it is `nil` until the first row\n   242\t   is folded, and an empty batch leaves it as it was.\n   243\t4. **Advance** `:batch`, and `:ids` in `:unresolved` mode.\n   244\t\n   245\tThe `:indexes` after batch *n* are a pure function of (index state before,\n   246\tbatch), on every host.\n   247\t\n   248\t### 2.3 What the two views mean over each medium\n   340\t### 3.2 Resolution facts\n   341\t\n   342\tFor every tempid `τ` the observer maps to durable `δ` in batch *n* —\n   343\twhichever slot it appeared in — it asserts, in its own attribute namespace:\n   344\t\n   345\t```\n   346\t[δ :dao.space.index/batch   n   n default-op]\n   347\t[δ :dao.space.index/tempid  τ   n default-op]\n   348\t```\n   349\t\n   350\tThe row's `t` is the observer's batch ordinal *n* — its own logical clock,\n   351\tnever a host clock, and not the observed rows' `t`. `m` is\n   352\t`datom/default-op`: the attribute namespace alone distinguishes these facts\n   353\tfrom observed rows, and nothing else is needed. That is a **contract on\n   354\tmedia, not a check in the index**: a medium must not assert attributes in\n   355\t`:dao.space.index/*`. The index does not inspect attribute namespaces\n   356\t(decision 6), so a medium that violates the reservation shares `[e a v]`\n   357\tkeys with resolution facts and owns the resulting `current` semantics\n   358\t(greatest-`t` shadowing, or the `m`-conflict throw on a same-`t` meet).\n   359\tWith the reservation honoured, resolution facts and observed rows have\n   360\tdisjoint `[e a v]` keys and can neither shadow nor conflict. These are\n   361\tfacts *about* observation, distinct from the observed rows. They make\n   362\t\"which batch asserted this entity\" — `[?e :dao.space.index/batch ?n]` — a\n   363\tquery, and they are the raw material for cross-medium provenance.\n   364\t\n   365\t**Refs are batch-local; cross-medium coordinates are values.** Refs are\n   366\tresolved only for attributes the supplied `:schema` declares as\n   367\t`:db.type/ref` — exactly as the transactor relocates only declared refs\n   368\ttoday — and a declared ref *always* resolves within the batch it appears\n   369\tin. There is no way, at attribute granularity, to say \"this `-16` names an\n   370\tentity on another medium\": the index would allocate a batch-local entity\n   371\tfor it, and a shared allocator only prevents *collisions*, it establishes\n   372\tno *correspondence*. So a coordinate into another medium must be carried as\n   373\ta **value**, qualified by the batch it refers to, under an attribute the\n   374\tschema does not declare a ref; the join is then value-based over\n   375\tresolution facts — `[?e :dao.space.index/batch t'] [?e\n   376\t:dao.space.index/tempid -16]` — and is the composition's, since only the\n   377\tcomposition knows which observer's ordinal `t'` corresponds to the\n   378\tproducer's batch number. That correspondence is a constant offset **only\n   379\twhile neither session has seen a `gap`**; a gap count records that gaps\n   380\toccurred, not how many batches were lost, so after one the offset must be\n   381\tre-derived or provenance across it marked unknown (open question 2).\n   382\t\n   383\tThis has a consequence for `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1, recorded here as a\n   384\t**required amendment** to that document: its `:yin/source-call` is written\n   385\tas both an external coordinate (initial expansion: an eid in the source\n   386\tbatch) and a log-local ref (nested expansion: a node in the log copy). One\n   387\tattribute cannot be both. It must split — an undeclared value attribute for\n   388\tthe external coordinate, qualified by `:yin/source-batch` as it already is,\n   389\tand a declared-ref attribute for the log-local node — so that\n   390\t`event-schema` declares only the latter a ref and the index resolves it\n   391\tbatch-locally while leaving the former as the value the join needs. The\n   392\tschema is the composition's to supply (for a program medium,\n   393\t`yin.vm/schema`; for an expander's log, the amended `event-schema`); the\n   394\tindex learns which attributes are refs from it and nothing else. An\n   395\tundeclared attribute holding a negative number is a value, not a ref, and is\n   396\tleft alone.\n   397\t\n   398\t### 3.3 What is not promised\n   399\t\n   400\tDurable ids are the *observer's* reading. Two independent observers over the\n   401\tsame medium allocate independently; their ids agree only if they are the\n   402\tsame session or share a checkpoint (§4.2). This is the same statement as\n   403\t`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2's \"refs across media are meaningful only for durable\n   404\tids\", now with the owner of \"durable\" named: it is whichever dao.space\n   405\tobserver indexed the medium.\n   665\town docstring says it inspects no evaluator field, and a `dao.space`\n   666\tnamespace requiring `yin.vm.*` would invert the layering (`dao.stream` →\n   667\t`dao.space` → `yin.vm`, never back). It is now `dao.stream.observer`\n   668\t(`src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc`, test\n   669\t`test/dao/stream/observer_test.cljc`), beside `dao.stream.observe`\n   670\twhose single `step` it loops over. The `yin.vm` evaluators, the REPL, and\n   671\tthe macro expander require it from there; `yin.vm` keeps no observer code\n   672\tof its own. The pending fix from `yin.vm.macro.md` §5 — keep the throw,\n   673\tcarry the partial `{:observer :consumer}` session in `ex-data` — lands in this\n   674\tnamespace.\n   675\t\n   676\t**Phase 0′ — parity over the transactor's medium.** *Gated on the\n   677\t`run-on-stream` partial-session fix landing first* (Phase 0's pending item):\n   678\ta round that finishes a staged publication and then throws on a later read\n   679\tor fold would otherwise hand the caller its pre-round session, and a retry\n   680\twould repeat the accepted publication. `dao.space.index` gains `fold-batch`,\n   681\t`publish!`, `flush-staged`, `drain`, `db-value`, `checkpoint`, `restore`,\n   682\tthe admitting element rule and outer grammar, and the resolution facts; a\n   683\t`:resolved` session attached at `:oldest` over a transactor's local\n   684\tmemory-log produces trees **logically equal** to `index-datoms` over\n   685\t`snapshot-datoms` of the same log — the same rows, counts, and query results\n   686\tover `db-value`, and a valid restore of what it publishes. *Not* an\n   687\tidentical manifest: `publish-index!` bulk-builds with `from-sequential`\n   688\twhile the session inserts with `conj`, and the two partition the same rows\n   689\tdifferently (a JVM probe at branching factor 4 confirms equal rows, unequal\n   690\troot blobs); canonical layout would be a new design requirement, not a\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
