{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '595,625p;228,250p;990,1005p' && nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md | sed -n '248,280p;650,690p'\"","aggregated_output":"   228\t|         | nil, booleans, numbers, strings, |                                                                                                                           |\n   229\t|         | keywords, symbols, and           |                                                                                                                           |\n   230\t|         | collections of them, with plain  |                                                                                                                           |\n   231\t|         | metadata                         |                                                                                                                           |\n   232\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   233\t| `key`   | a store key: any value of the    | the store contract is a map lookup with no key restriction (`ast_walker.cljc:401`, `:402-404`); the linearizer admits any |\n   234\t|         | `data` kind                      | `plain-data?` key (`linearize.cljc:79-85`, `:138-141`) and the loader carries it through unchanged                        |\n   235\t|         |                                  | (`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:577-579`), so symbols (`yin/def`), keywords (`test/yin/vm/parity_test.cljc:99`), and|\n   236\t|         |                                  | numbers are all portable keys today. `key` is a named kind rather than `data` only so that the same definition is cited by|\n   237\t|         |                                  | AST validation (§7.4), instruction validation (§7.5), dependency extraction (§7.7), and the store-slice encoding of UCF   |\n   238\t|         |                                  | §7.6.2; it is the `data` domain and nothing narrower                                                                      |\n   239\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   240\t| `sym`   | one symbol                       |                                                                                                                           |\n   241\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   242\t| `kw`    | one keyword                      |                                                                                                                           |\n   243\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   244\t| `str`   | one string                       |                                                                                                                           |\n   245\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   246\t| `int`   | one non-negative integer         |                                                                                                                           |\n   247\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   248\t| `bool`  | exactly `true` or `false`        | never nil, never absent                                                                                                   |\n   249\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   250\t\n   595\t\n   596\t### 4.3 Every node is addressed by construction\n   597\t\n   598\tPer-node addressing is not a projection consequence and no longer a\n   599\tfollow-up: a row's id is its node's address by construction (§2.1), so\n   600\tevery node of every tree is named, shareable, and queryable the moment it\n   601\tis projected. The round-7 question — a `:lambda` whose body is\n   602\t*materialized separately*, referred to by address from the canonical form\n   603\tof the enclosing tree, pinned by manifests on its own — is the ordinary\n   604\tshape of the stored row DAG, not a canonical-form extension: every child\n   605\tslot already refers by address, and a manifest may name any row's id. What\n   606\tremains of it is a lowering question — one segment per lambda rather than\n   607\tout-of-line bodies in one segment (`linearize.cljc:151-158`) — which is a\n   608\t§5.2.1 profile concern, changes no row address, and is not open here.\n   609\t\n   610\t### 4.4 Sharing by content address replaces `:eid`; occurrences stay outside\n   611\t\n   612\tToday a shared subtree is emitted once and referenced by a pre-assigned\n   613\tentity id: `yang.clojure` allocates one for each defmacro lambda\n   614\t(`clojure.cljc:418-437`) and the codec deduplicates on it\n   615\t(`v2.cljc:394-411`). Its purpose is that the definition operand and each\n   616\tcall-site operator resolve to one entity so provenance can point at it.\n   617\t\n   618\tContent addressing gives that for free and in every case, not only the one\n   619\t`yang.clojure` remembers to mark: two occurrences of one subtree have one\n   620\taddress by construction and one row in the stored set, and a query \"which\n   621\tsites reference this lambda's content\" is a join on its address (§6.4).\n   622\tBecause names are excluded from the canonical tuple (§2.5), this structural\n   623\tsharing is alpha-equivalent sharing: structurally identical functions up to\n   624\tvariable renaming project to the identical row. The row set holds each distinct\n   625\tsubtree once, and parent slots point at it — while identity stays per row.\n   990\t| `:saturation`     | a saturated slot (§2.4) is nil                                                                                                                    |\n   991\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   992\t| `:id-resolves`    | a child id in a `node` or `nodes` slot names no row of the loaded set                                                                             |\n   993\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   994\t| `:acyclic`        | following child ids from a row revisits a row already on its own path                                                                             |\n   995\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   996\t| `:root-reachable` | a row of the loaded set is not reachable from the root row                                                                                        |\n   997\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   998\t| `:variable-bounds`| a `:variable` name must be bound by an enclosing `:lambda`'s params, else the frontend must have emitted `:global`; the defect names the path |\n   999\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1000\t\n  1001\tThe three reference rules are the row counterpart of §7.5's\n  1002\t`:target-bounds`: a row's child slots are references, and they must\n  1003\tresolve within the loaded set and close under the root, as a jump target\n  1004\tmust land inside its segment. Acyclicity is also implied by the per-row\n  1005\taddress check whenever it runs — a row's id commits to its children's\n   248\t### 2.3 What the two views mean over each medium\n   249\t\n   250\t`dao.space.query/current` and `history` are unchanged: they are\n   251\tinterpreters over rows, and the index state's trees are a row source.\n   252\t\n   253\t| Medium | `t` in rows | `current` | `history` |\n   254\t|---|---|---|---|\n   255\t| transactor-written (records) | allocated by the writer, monotonic | greatest-`t`-wins per `[e a v]`, retractions removed — full Datomic semantics | the exact rows; `history` promises no ordering — over a `db-value` the rows come in EAVT-walk order, not writer order — so order by `t` explicitly when it matters |\n   256\t| a medium whose rows carry no writer-allocated `t` (all rows `t 0`, `m default-op`) | not meaningful | the *set* of facts asserted — correct for a medium of assertions with no retractions | degenerate: all rows share `t 0`; order is the observer's batch ordinal, available through resolution facts (§3), **not** through `t` |\n   257\t\n   258\tThe observer does not paper over the second row by minting `t` — decision\n   259\t4. A composition that wants transaction-time semantics over such a medium\n   260\troutes it through a transactor (the writer allocates `t`); one that wants\n   261\t\"what facts does this medium assert\" reads `current` and gets exactly that.\n   262\t`as-of` filters *both* observed rows and resolution facts numerically\n   263\tagainst `t`, though one carries writer time and the other the observer's\n   264\tordinal; a composition that uses `as-of` over a session's trees is\n   265\tfiltering two clocks with one number and should say which it means.\n   266\t**Retractions on such a medium**: an assertion and a later retraction of\n   267\tthe same `[e a v]` — the same *resolved* `e`; a reused negative tempid in a\n   268\tlater batch is a different entity and conflicts with nothing — both carry\n   269\t`t 0` and differ only in `m`, which\n   270\t`current-state-seq` rejects as conflicting history (`query.cljc`, the\n   271\t`m`-conflict throw). The index does not check for this — deciding whether\n   272\tan `m` value means \"retract\" is payload interpretation, decision 6 — so the\n   273\trows are folded and `history` answers correctly, while `current` over such\n   274\ta medium *throws* at query time. Loud, not silent; a composition that\n   275\tretracts on a medium without writer `t` has chosen the wrong medium for\n   276\t`current`.\n   277\t\n   278\t---\n   279\t\n   280\t## 3. Identity\n   650\t|---|---|\n   651\t| No hidden global state | Allocator, batch ordinal, trees, staged publication — all fields of one index-state value threaded through `run-on-stream`. No registry of observers; a composition holds the states it created. |\n   652\t| No implicit control flow | Indexing happens in exactly one place: `fold-batch` inside the observe step. Nothing indexes on write, on query, or on publish. |\n   653\t| No callbacks | `fold-batch` is a function (index-state, batch) → index-state'. The driver is `run-on-stream`; cadence is the composition's. |\n   654\t| No shared mutable state | `dao.data.btree` values are persistent; two sessions never share a tree by reference they could both mutate. The recording handle's identity persists with the session and its content is drained at each accepted manifest (§4.1); a shared allocator is a value threaded serially, never an atom (§3.1). |\n   655\t| No layer collapsing | *append* (writer) → medium → *observe/fold* (this note) → *publish* (intake) → *materialize* (`dao.jing`) → *query*. The VM is a sibling observer, not a stage. |\n   656\t| No assumed graphs | Ref resolution uses only the supplied `:schema`; the observer does not infer which values are refs. Batch shape is validated by the admitting per-element rule and the outer grammar of §2.2 step 0; malformed input is a defect, not a guess. |\n   657\t\n   658\t---\n   659\t\n   660\t## 7. Phases\n   661\t\n   662\t**Phase 0 — relocate the coordination loop (done 2026-09-13).** The\n   663\t`attach`/`observe-next`/`run-on-stream` loop lived at\n   664\t`yin.vm.stream-observer` because the VM was its first consumer, but its\n   665\town docstring says it inspects no evaluator field, and a `dao.space`\n   666\tnamespace requiring `yin.vm.*` would invert the layering (`dao.stream` →\n   667\t`dao.space` → `yin.vm`, never back). It is now `dao.stream.observer`\n   668\t(`src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc`, test\n   669\t`test/dao/stream/observer_test.cljc`), beside `dao.stream.observe`\n   670\twhose single `step` it loops over. The `yin.vm` evaluators, the REPL, and\n   671\tthe macro expander require it from there; `yin.vm` keeps no observer code\n   672\tof its own. The pending fix from `yin.vm.macro.md` §5 — keep the throw,\n   673\tcarry the partial `{:observer :consumer}` session in `ex-data` — lands in this\n   674\tnamespace.\n   675\t\n   676\t**Phase 0′ — parity over the transactor's medium.** *Gated on the\n   677\t`run-on-stream` partial-session fix landing first* (Phase 0's pending item):\n   678\ta round that finishes a staged publication and then throws on a later read\n   679\tor fold would otherwise hand the caller its pre-round session, and a retry\n   680\twould repeat the accepted publication. `dao.space.index` gains `fold-batch`,\n   681\t`publish!`, `flush-staged`, `drain`, `db-value`, `checkpoint`, `restore`,\n   682\tthe admitting element rule and outer grammar, and the resolution facts; a\n   683\t`:resolved` session attached at `:oldest` over a transactor's local\n   684\tmemory-log produces trees **logically equal** to `index-datoms` over\n   685\t`snapshot-datoms` of the same log — the same rows, counts, and query results\n   686\tover `db-value`, and a valid restore of what it publishes. *Not* an\n   687\tidentical manifest: `publish-index!` bulk-builds with `from-sequential`\n   688\twhile the session inserts with `conj`, and the two partition the same rows\n   689\tdifferently (a JVM probe at branching factor 4 confirms equal rows, unequal\n   690\troot blobs); canonical layout would be a new design requirement, not a\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"# Definitive architecture verdict: REJECT\n\nThe architecture is moving in the right direction, but the finalized plan is not yet mathematically coherent or implementation-ready.\n\nThe two V4 concerns have been addressed conceptually, but not completely in the normative documents:\n\n- Named variables are now the intended semantic representation, but De Bruijn/positional rules remain in several contract-defining sections.\n- `dao.space.index :unresolved` is the correct place to resolve batch-local tempids, but its resolution facts do not by themselves provide the “perfect idempotency” claimed by the plan.\n\n## Blocking findings\n\n### P0 — There is still no single canonical variable grammar\n\nThe main dictionary correctly specifies:\n\n- `[:variable name]`\n- `[:lambda params body]`\n\nat [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:268](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:268).\n\nHowever, the same document still specifies or assumes:\n\n- Map AST `:arity` and `:index` at [line 148](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:148).\n- Tuple rows `[:lambda 1 B]` and `[:variable 0]` at [line 168](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:168).\n- Positional-environment adaptation at [line 156](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:156).\n- A variable-name side table based on binding indices at [line 423](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:423).\n- `:var` index and `:closure` arity validation at [line 1028](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1028).\n- A captured “positional environment” at [line 1212](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1212).\n- “Positional frame binding” in the migration table at [line 1743](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1743).\n\nThe alpha-equivalence claim is also now false. The document says variable names are excluded and renamed lambdas obtain the same address at [line 622](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:622), while the canonical dictionary explicitly hashes variable names and parameter lists.\n\nMore importantly, the execution contract disagrees over name resolution:\n\n- `yin.vm.semantic` defines `:var` as `env → store → primitives → modules` at [yin.vm.semantic.md:160](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:160).\n- `code-as-tuples` defines distinct `:variable` and `:global` nodes and says an unbound variable evaluates to `nil`, never falling through, at [line 1188](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1188).\n- Current frontends emit only `:variable`, including globals such as `yin/def`; the plan does not assign responsibility for classifying them into `:variable` versus `:global`.\n\nConsequently, the claimed `ast-walker → rows → semantic VM` semantic equivalence is not currently defined.\n\nRequired decision: publish one exact grammar and resolution law across frontend, walker, row validator, lowerer, vector validator, and semantic VM. If `:global` remains distinct, the plan must name which stage performs lexical classification and how round-trip identity is defined after that classification.\n\nThe lambda `params` slot also needs a proper kind—normally a vector of symbols—and explicit rules for duplicates. Calling it unrestricted `data` is insufficient for binding validation and future Register VM lowering.\n\n### P0 — Content addressing remains explicitly blocked\n\nThe governing design states that `dao.jing` currently has non-injective normalization: lists, seqs, vectors, and metadata-bearing values can collapse to the same encoded value. It explicitly blocks rows, DAG sharing, address identity, deduplication, and ledger references at [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1815](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1815).\n\nThe plan’s workaround—having the AST indexer avoid collapsing equal IDs “pending the `dao.jing` fix” at [the plan:65](/Users/sto/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/d463453f-497a-4a75-a0bd-20cd63ac6838/vm_semantic_bytecode_migration_plan.md:65)—is not sound. If two different row bodies have the same ID:\n\n- child references become ambiguous;\n- root reconstruction is not a function;\n- `$ast` cannot use the ID as a relational key;\n- the Merkle and round-trip claims fail.\n\nThe canonical encoder must be fixed first, or the initial implementation must use a deliberately non-addressed prototype representation and make no identity or deduplication claims.\n\n### P0 — Resolution facts do not guarantee append-level idempotency\n\nThe index transition is deterministic:\n\n\\[\nF(S_n,B_n)=S_{n+1}\n\\]\n\nRecomputing it from the same pre-state produces the same allocation. That is deterministic replay.\n\nBut applying the same payload after the first result has been committed processes it as another observer batch:\n\n\\[\nF(F(S_n,B),B)\\ne F(S_n,B)\n\\]\n\nThe second occurrence receives a new batch ordinal and fresh durable IDs. That is intentional under the batch-local identity contract.\n\nThe resolution facts at [dao.space.index.as-observer.md:342](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md:342) record `(batch, tempid) → durable-id`; they do not detect or suppress a duplicate producer append. Therefore the statement at [the plan:66](/Users/sto/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/d463453f-497a-4a75-a0bd-20cd63ac6838/vm_semantic_bytecode_migration_plan.md:66) is too strong.\n\nRetry safety actually depends on:\n\n- retaining the post-progress observer session;\n- advancing the stream cursor exactly once;\n- restoring cursor, allocator, and batch ordinal together;\n- preserving staged-output progress.\n\nThe index design itself makes the `run-on-stream` partial-session fix a prerequisite because otherwise an accepted publication can be repeated on retry at [dao.space.index.as-observer.md:676](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md:676).\n\nThe plan must distinguish:\n\n1. retry before committing the fold;\n2. retry after committing the fold but before cursor persistence;\n3. process restart from a verified checkpoint;\n4. an identical payload appended twice by the producer.\n\nOnly the first and properly checkpointed third cases are deterministic replay. Case four is not idempotent without a stable producer batch identity plus a seen-batch/fingerprint rule.\n\n### P0 — The encoder’s two outputs have no consistency boundary\n\nThe encoder writes one logical input batch to two separate media:\n\n- AST rows;\n- side-table datoms.\n\nNo atomic commit or recoverable dual-output state machine is specified. A crash can leave:\n\n- AST rows present but metadata absent;\n- metadata present but AST rows absent;\n- one output duplicated while the other is not.\n\nWaiting until both observers are “caught up” does not prove they cover the same logical source batches.\n\nThis is especially important because `:dao.space.index/batch` is observer-local, not the producer’s batch identity. The index design explicitly warns that cross-medium ordinal correspondence becomes invalid after a gap at [dao.space.index.as-observer.md:365](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md:365).\n\nBoth outputs need a stable producer-owned batch coordinate—preferably including medium identity and a content fingerprint—and the encoder needs per-output staged progress or an outbox protocol.\n\n### P1 — Side-table assertions and the mutable ledger are conflated\n\nBypassing `dao.space.transactor` is sound for immutable assertion-only side tables with batch-local tempids.\n\nIt is not equivalent to a full Datomic-style ledger. On an unresolved medium with `t = 0`:\n\n- history has no writer transaction time;\n- assertion/retraction pairs can make `current` throw;\n- `as-of` mixes writer time with observer batch ordinals.\n\nThose limitations are explicit at [dao.space.index.as-observer.md:253](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md:253).\n\nMeanwhile, the tuple design still says mutable refs, naming history, and ledger events belong to a transactor-owned datom path at [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1799](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1799).\n\nThe plan should rename `Rel2` to an assertion-only side-table index, or separately specify how mutable ledger facts receive writer-owned transaction time.\n\n### P1 — The plan omits mandatory topology and implementation gates\n\nThe architecture document itself still identifies unresolved gates:\n\n- `dao.jing` canonical encoding;\n- AST-medium batch shape;\n- complete occurrence identity;\n- macro admission and expansion;\n- lowering profile publication;\n- observer partial-session recovery;\n- kept-cursor restore.\n\nThe plan also omits:\n\n- `dao.jing` materialization of the canonical rows;\n- a macro-expander stage before evaluator execution;\n- explicit behavior for programs containing macro definitions.\n\nIf the first implementation phase is intentionally macro-free and non-persistent, that scope must be stated. As written, the diagram presents a complete production pipeline.\n\n## What is architecturally sound\n\nThe following decisions should be retained:\n\n- Yang emits language-semantic Map ASTs rather than storage artifacts.\n- The walker and semantic VM are sibling observers, not coupled to one another.\n- `ast->semantic-bytecode` is the correct explicit AST-to-row boundary.\n- Semantic rows and semantic instructions should retain named variables.\n- De Bruijn conversion belongs in the later Register VM compiler.\n- A dedicated AST indexer is preferable to teaching `dao.space.index` about AST semantics.\n- `dao.space.index :unresolved` correctly gives negative tempids batch-local scope and resolves `e`, declared-reference `v`, and `m`.\n- Resolution facts are useful provenance and enable value-based joins.\n\n## Required acceptance tests\n\nBefore approval, the verification plan should add:\n\n- Walker versus semantic-VM behavioral parity, not merely serialization round-trip.\n- Lexical shadowing, nested closures, same-name globals and locals, unbound variables, duplicate parameters, and under/over-arity calls.\n- Canonical-encoding collision cases already listed in the design.\n- Encoder failures between each of its two output writes, including restart.\n- Same-batch retry versus duplicate producer append.\n- Checkpoint restore of cursor, allocator, batch ordinal, rejected count, and gap count.\n- Tempids occurring only in `v` or `m`, overlapping tempids across batches, and schema-declared versus ordinary negative values.\n- Cross-source joins after an ingress gap.\n- Explicit macro-free rejection or complete macro-expansion integration.\n\n## Final assessment\n\nThe broad stream topology is compatible with datom.world’s axioms and preserves the valuable “one medium, independent interpreters” model. The named-semantic/Register-VM separation is also the correct direction.\n\nHowever, the current documents do not yet define one consistent semantics, one safe content identity, or one valid retry theorem. Therefore the pipeline is not mathematically sound as currently stated and should not enter implementation as the finalized architecture.\n\nApproval should follow only after the canonical grammar is made internally consistent, `dao.jing` encoding is fixed, retry guarantees are precisely scoped, and the two-output correlation/commit protocol is specified."}}
